window.PROTOCOL_DATA=[{"name":"5-Amino-1MQ","protocolName":"5-Amino-1MQ","category":"Weight Loss / Metabolic","overview":"5-Amino-1MQ is a small molecule compound that blocks an enzyme called NNMT (nicotinamide N-methyltransferase). Unlike most compounds in this series, it is not a peptide. It is a small molecule, which means it has different properties, most notably that it survives your digestive system intact and can be taken orally.\n\nThe compound was developed by researchers at the University of Texas Medical Branch who were studying new approaches to obesity and metabolic dysfunction. In animal studies, mice treated with 5-Amino-1MQ lost significant body fat without any reduction in food intake. The fat loss came entirely from increased energy expenditure at the cellular level. This is a fundamentally different mechanism than GLP-1 drugs like semaglutide or retatrutide, which work primarily through appetite suppression.\n\n5-Amino-1MQ is a research compound. It has not been approved by the FDA for human use. No human clinical trials have been published. All efficacy data comes from preclinical animal models and in vitro studies.","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nNo published dose-finding studies exist for human use. The following protocols are extrapolated from animal data, pharmacokinetic modeling, and clinical practice patterns.\n\nORAL DOSING\nDose:           50 to 100 mg daily …","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":50,"unit":"mg","mg":50}],"strengths":[5,10,50]},{"name":"Adipotide","protocolName":"Adipotide","category":"Weight Loss / Metabolic","overview":"Adipotide is a synthetic peptide that destroys fat cells by cutting off their blood supply. Its full chemical name is CKGGRAKDC-GG-D(KLAKLAK)2, and it is also called FTPP (Fat Targeted Proapoptotic Peptide) or Prohibitin-TP01.\n\nUnlike every other weight loss compound you have heard of, adipotide does not suppress appetite, increase metabolic rate, or change how your body burns fuel. It targets the blood vessels that feed white adipose tissue and triggers programmed cell death in those vessels. When the blood supply dies, the fat cells starve and die with it.\n\nThe compound was originally developed as a cancer treatment. The idea was to starve tumors by destroying the blood vessels that feed them. Researchers at MD Anderson Cancer Center noticed that animals treated with the peptide lost dramatic amounts of body fat, and they pivoted to exploring it for obesity. The original mouse study was published in 2004. A primate study followed in 2011. A Phase 1 human trial was initiated in 2012 but was terminated before completion, and no results were ever published.\n\nThis is one of the most mechanistically interesting compounds in the peptide space. It is also one of the riskiest. The same mechanism that makes it effective at destroying fat tissue creates real safety concerns, particularly for the kidneys. As of 2026, there is zero published human efficacy or safety data. Everything we know comes from mouse studies, primate studies, and a handful of anecdotal reports from people who have used research grade material on their own.","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published human data. They are extrapolated from primate studies and represent the limited community discussion that exists. This compound carries substantially higher risk than most peptides due to its mechanism of action and confirmed kidney toxicity in primates.\n\nCONSERVATIVE PROTOCOL\nDose:           0.5 mg/kg body weight per day\nFrequency:      Once daily\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection\nDuration:       4 weeks maximum\nRecovery:       At least 4 weeks off between cycles\nBY BODY WEIGHT (conservative dose)\n150 lbs (68 kg): approximately 34 mg daily\n180 lbs (82 kg): approximately 41 mg daily\n200 lbs (91 kg): approximately 45 mg daily\n220 lbs (100 kg): approximately 50 mg daily\nIMPORTANT CONTEXT\nThe primate study identified 0.43 mg/kg as the optimal dose.\nKidney toxicity was observed above 0.25 mg/kg.\nThat means the \"therapeutic\" dose is already ABOVE the dose\nwhere kidney effects start appearing in primates.\nThere is no way to separate the fat loss from the kidney risk\nat effective doses based on current data.\nHydration\nStay extremely well hydrated during any use. The primate study noted dehydration at higher doses, and the kidneys are under stress from this compound by design. Inadequate hydration could amplify renal toxicity.\n\nMonitoring …","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":0.5,"unit":"mg","mg":0.5}],"strengths":[2,5,10]},{"name":"AICAR","protocolName":"AICAR","category":"Weight Loss / Metabolic","overview":"AICAR stands for 5-aminoimidazole-4-carboxamide ribonucleoside. It is also known by its pharmaceutical name acadesine. It is a synthetic analog of adenosine, a molecule your body naturally produces during normal purine metabolism.\n\nYour body makes AICAR naturally as an intermediate step in the de novo purine biosynthesis pathway, the process your cells use to build adenosine and other nucleotides from scratch. The synthetic version mimics this naturally occurring intermediate and activates the same metabolic switch that exercise turns on, a master enzyme called AMPK (AMP-activated protein kinase).\n\nIn 2008, researchers at the Salk Institute published what became one of the most talked about exercise science papers of the decade. They gave sedentary mice AICAR for four weeks and those mice ran 44% further on an endurance test without ever training. The compound had activated the same metabolic genes that exercise turns on, essentially giving the mice an endurance boost they never earned. Media outlets ran with the headline \"exercise in a pill.\"\n\nThe sports world noticed fast. The French Anti-Doping Agency suspected AICAR use during the 2009 Tour de France. By 2011, WADA officially banned it. In 2012, Spanish police arrested ten people in an operation codenamed Operacion Skype for trafficking AICAR and TB-500 to professional athletes, calling them \"next generation superdrugs.\"\n\nAICAR also has a history in cardiac surgery research. In the 1990s, five large randomized clinical trials involving over 4,000 patients tested AICAR (under the name acadesine) for preventing heart damage during coronary artery bypass surgery. Those are the largest human datasets that exist for this compound.\n\nAICAR is not FDA approved for human use. …","schedule":"Practical Protocols\n\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent community consensus and practical application patterns. The disconnect between studied doses and community doses should be clearly understood before using this compound.\n\nCONSERVATIVE PROTOCOL\n\nDose:           10 to 25 mg daily\n\nRoute:          Subcutaneous injection\n\nDuration:       2 to 4 weeks\n\nFrequency:      Daily or every other day\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\n\nDose:           25 to 50 mg daily\n\nRoute:          Subcutaneous injection\n\nDuration:       2 weeks maximum\n\nFrequency:      Daily\n\nALTERNATIVE PROTOCOL\n\nDose:           50 mg every other day\n\nDuration:       2 weeks (7 doses total)\n\nNote:           Same total exposure as daily dosing over shorter period\n\nLOW-DOSE METABOLIC PROTOCOL\n\nDose:           2.5 to 5 mg daily\n\nRoute:          Subcutaneous injection\n\nDuration:       4 to 8 weeks\n\nNote:           Used by some for metabolic support alongside\n\nother AMPK-targeting compounds like MOTS-c.\n\nNo published data supports this dose range.\n\nCycle Guidelines\n\nMaximum cycle length is generally kept to 14 days at standard doses (25 to 50 mg daily). At lower doses (2.5 to 10 mg), some run longer cycles of 4 to 8 weeks. A washout period of 1 to 2 months between cycles is standard practice. No more than 3 cycles per year at standard doses. Monitor kidney function and blood sugar throughout use.\n\nReconstitution\n\nRECONSTITUTION (standard) …","frequency":"Every other day","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":10,"unit":"mg","mg":10},{"value":25,"unit":"mg","mg":25},{"value":2.5,"unit":"mg","mg":2.5}],"strengths":[50,100]},{"name":"AOD-9604","protocolName":"AOD-9604","category":"Weight Loss / Metabolic","overview":"AOD-9604 stands for Anti-Obesity Drug 9604. It is a synthetic peptide consisting of 16 amino acids derived from the tail end of human growth hormone, specifically amino acids 176 through 191 with a tyrosine substitution at the N-terminus to stabilize the molecule.\n\nThe idea behind it was straightforward. Growth hormone is one of the most powerful fat-burning hormones in the body, but it comes with a list of side effects: insulin resistance, water retention, elevated IGF-1 (which can accelerate tumor growth), carpal tunnel, and in extreme cases organ growth. Researchers at Monash University in Australia asked a smart question: what if you could isolate just the fat-burning piece and strip away everything else? So they cut out those C-terminal amino acids and engineered a standalone fragment that, in theory, would give you the lipolytic benefit of growth hormone without the downsides.\n\nAOD-9604 went through extensive clinical development. It completed six human clinical trials with over 900 participants in total. It was developed by Metabolic Pharmaceuticals Ltd., an Australian biotech company. The safety data was consistently excellent, but the weight loss results were underwhelming. The largest Phase 2b trial (the OPTIONS study, 536 obese adults over 24 weeks) failed to achieve statistically significant weight loss versus placebo. Metabolic Pharmaceuticals terminated the obesity program in 2007.\n\nDespite that clinical failure, AOD-9604 remains widely available through research peptide suppliers and TRT clinics. The safety profile makes it low-risk to sell, the cost is low, the name sounds clinical and intentional, and it received GRAS (Generally Recognized as Safe) status for food applications. But being safe and being effective are two different questions. …","schedule":"Standard Protocol:\n\n300 mcg once daily, first thing in the morning on an empty stomach\n\nWait at least 30 minutes before eating\n\nHigher Dose Protocol:\n\n500 mcg once daily on an empty stomach\n\nSome users split this into 250 mcg twice daily (morning and before bed)\n\nCycle Length:\n\n4 to 12 weeks is typical\n\nMany users take a break of equal length before repeating\n\nImportant Notes:\n\nThe empty stomach timing matters. Food in your system may interfere with absorption and effectiveness.\n\nMorning dosing is most common because it aligns with natural lipolytic rhythms and fasted states.\n\nDo not expect dramatic results. The clinical trial data showed modest effects at best. AOD-9604 may provide a small additional boost when combined with proper diet and exercise, but it is not a primary fat loss tool.\n\nDraw Volumes by Vial Size\nAll calculations assume reconstitution volumes that provide practical measurements on a standard insulin syringe.\n\n5 mg Vial (2 mL reconstitution = 2.5 mg/mL or 2500 mcg/mL)\n\nDose        Volume       Units on Syringe\n250 mcg     0.10 mL      10 units\n300 mcg     0.12 mL      12 units\n500 mcg     0.20 mL      20 units\n\nVial duration at 300 mcg/day: approximately 16 days\nVial duration at 500 mcg/day: 10 days\n\nThis is the recommended reconstitution for practical daily dosing.\n\n5 mg Vial (1 mL reconstitution = 5 mg/mL or 5000 mcg/mL)\n\nDose        Volume       Units on Syringe\n250 mcg     0.05 mL      5 units\n300 mcg     0.06 mL      6 units\n500 mcg     0.10 mL      10 units\n\nVial duration at 300 mcg/day: approximately 16 days\nVial duration at 500 mcg/day: 10 days\n\nThis concentration works but the volumes are very small\nand harder to measure precisely.\n\nSide Effects and Cautions\nAOD-9604 has a favorable safety profile based on clinical trial data. Side effects were minimal and comparable to placebo in most studies.\n\nPotential Effects:\n\nInjection site reactions (redness, mild irritation)\n\nHeadache (rare)\n\nMild nausea (rare)\n\nIndigestion (rare) …","frequency":"Twice daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":300,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.3},{"value":500,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.5},{"value":250,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.25}],"strengths":[2,5,10]},{"name":"ARA-290","protocolName":"ARA-290","category":"Recovery / Repair","overview":"ARA-290 is a synthetic 11 amino acid peptide derived from the tissue protective domain of erythropoietin (EPO). Its sequence is Pyr-Glu-Glu-Leu-Glu-Arg-Ala-Leu-Asn-Ser-Ser. It is also known as cibinetide and helix B surface peptide (HBSP). Its molecular weight is 1257 Da.\n\nIf you have heard of EPO, you know it as the hormone your kidneys produce to stimulate red blood cell production. Athletes have abused it for decades to increase oxygen carrying capacity. But EPO also has powerful tissue protective and anti-inflammatory effects that are completely separate from its blood building activity. The problem is that when you give someone EPO for tissue protection, you also get all the dangerous blood effects: elevated hematocrit, increased clotting risk, hypertension, and cardiovascular complications.\n\nResearchers identified that EPO's tissue protective effects are mediated by a different receptor than its blood building effects. They mapped the specific region of the EPO molecule responsible for tissue protection, the helix B surface domain, and engineered ARA-290 to mimic just that region. The result is a peptide that activates tissue repair and calms inflammation without touching red blood cell production, hematocrit, blood viscosity, or blood pressure.\n\nARA-290 has received FDA Orphan Drug and Fast Track designations for the treatment of sarcoidosis associated small fiber neuropathy. It also has EU Orphan Medicinal Product designation for prevention of graft loss in pancreatic islet transplantation. Multiple Phase 2 clinical trials have been completed with positive results across sarcoidosis neuropathy and diabetic neuropathy. It is not FDA approved for general use. It is available as a research chemical.","schedule":"Practical Protocol\n\nUnlike most peptides in the healing and recovery category, ARA-290 has a clinically established dosing protocol.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL (based on clinical trial data)\n\nDose:           4 mg daily\n\nRoute:          Subcutaneous injection\n\nTiming:         Once daily, morning dosing used in most trials\n\nDuration:       28 days minimum (clinical trial standard)\n\nDiluent:        Phosphate buffered saline (PBS) preferred over bacteriostatic water\n\nTITRATION APPROACH (community practice)\n\nWeek 1:         2 mg daily (assess tolerance)\n\nWeek 2 to 4:    4 mg daily (full therapeutic dosing)\n\nRECONSTITUTION\n\nVial size:      10 mg\n\nPBS volume:     Follow manufacturer instructions for reconstitution\n\nStore:          Refrigerated after reconstitution\n\nImportant Considerations\n\nARA-290 does not require fasting. Timing relative to meals does not appear to significantly affect absorption based on clinical trial protocols.\n\nARA-290 does not require hematocrit monitoring (unlike EPO). No dose adjustments for kidney function were needed in studies, though one patient with pre-existing renal insufficiency showed worsening kidney function.\n\nThe clinical trial data consistently shows 28 days as the treatment period. For chronic conditions, longer courses may be warranted, but data beyond 28 days is limited. The sustained effects observed after stopping treatment (pain relief persisting months after a single week of IV dosing, metabolic improvements continuing 28 days after stopping) suggest that continuous indefinite use may not be necessary.","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":4,"unit":"mg","mg":4},{"value":2,"unit":"mg","mg":2}],"strengths":[10,16,30,40,50]},{"name":"BPC-157","protocolName":"BPC-157","category":"Recovery / Repair","overview":"BPC-157 stands for Body Protection Compound 157. It is a synthetic peptide consisting of 15 amino acids derived from a protective protein naturally found in human gastric juice. The sequence is Gly-Glu-Pro-Pro-Pro-Gly-Lys-Pro-Ala-Asp-Asp-Ala-Gly-Leu-Val.\n\nYour body naturally produces BPC in the stomach, where it plays a role in protecting and repairing the gastrointestinal lining. The synthetic version isolates the active fragment of this larger protein and has been studied extensively in animal models for its regenerative effects on tendons, ligaments, muscles, bones, and the gut.\n\nBPC-157 was first described in scientific literature in 1993 and has been the subject of over 100 preclinical studies since then. Despite all that research, human clinical data is extremely limited. As of early 2026, only three published human pilot studies exist.\n\nIt is not approved by the FDA for human use. In 2022, WADA banned it under the S0 Unapproved Substances category, and it is also banned by the UFC, NFL, NCAA, NHL, MLB, NAIA, and PGA. In 2023, the FDA classified it as a Category 2 bulk drug substance, effectively barring its use in compounded medications due to insufficient evidence on human safety.","schedule":"Practical Protocols\n\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\n\nDose:           250 to 500 mcg per day\n\nFrequency:      Once or twice daily\n\nTiming:         Morning and/or evening (no fasting required)\n\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection (preferred for localized injuries) or oral\n\nDuration:       4 to 8 weeks for active healing\n\nBY BODY WEIGHT\n\nUnder 150 lbs:  200 to 300 mcg daily\n\n150 to 200 lbs: 300 to 400 mcg daily\n\nOver 200 lbs:   400 to 500 mcg daily\n\nBY APPLICATION\n\nAcute injury recovery:     400 to 500 mcg daily for 2 to 4 weeks, then reduce\n\nChronic injury support:    250 to 300 mcg daily for 6 to 8 weeks\n\nGut healing:               250 to 500 mcg daily, oral administration preferred\n\nPost-surgical recovery:    300 to 400 mcg daily (with surgeon approval)\n\nRECONSTITUTION (standard)\n\nVial size:      5 mg\n\nBac water:      2 mL\n\nConcentration:  250 mcg per 10 units on insulin syringe\n\nWhere to Inject\n\nBPC-157 amplifies existing repair signals at damaged tissue rather than creating new ones. With a half-life of less than 30 minutes, injection proximity matters.\n\nFor a specific musculoskeletal injury, inject near the injury site for maximum concentration at the target tissue before it clears. For multiple injuries or systemic inflammation, subcutaneous anywhere works because BPC-157 still circulates and reaches damaged tissue throughout the body. …","frequency":"Twice daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":250,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.25}],"strengths":[2,5,10,20]},{"name":"BPC-157 + TB500","protocolName":"BPC-157 / TB-500 Blend","category":"Recovery / Repair","overview":"The Wolverine Stack is a pre-blended combination of two healing peptides: BPC-157 (Body Protection Compound 157) and TB-500 (a synthetic version of Thymosin Beta-4). The name comes from the Marvel character Wolverine, known for his superhuman ability to heal from virtually any injury. The concept is straightforward: two compounds that target different stages of the healing process, combined in a single vial so you only have to manage one reconstitution and one injection.\n\nBPC-157 is a synthetic 15 amino acid peptide derived from a protective protein naturally found in human gastric juice. It works primarily through growth factor signaling, nitric oxide modulation, and angiogenesis to promote localized tissue repair.\n\nTB-500 is a synthetic version of thymosin beta-4, a naturally occurring 43 amino acid protein found in nearly every human and animal cell. It works primarily through actin regulation, cell migration, and systemic tissue regeneration.\n\nEach vial contains 5 mg of BPC-157 and 5 mg of TB-500, totaling 10 mg. The 1:1 weight ratio delivers both peptides at commonly used therapeutic doses when injected at standard volumes.\n\nThe Wolverine Stack is not FDA approved for human use. Both BPC-157 and TB-500 are banned by the World Anti-Doping Agency under the S0 Unapproved Substances category. In 2023, the FDA classified both peptides as Category 2 bulk drug substances, effectively barring their use in compounded medications due to insufficient evidence on human safety. Both are also banned by the UFC, NFL, NCAA, NHL, MLB, NAIA, and PGA.\n\nI have personal experience with this stack. After a near-complete tricep tendon tear during a jiu-jitsu competition, I started using the Wolverine Stack immediately. …","schedule":"practical protocols below are not derived from published dose-finding research. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nPractical Protocols\n\nSTANDARD WOLVERINE STACK PROTOCOL (daily dosing)\n\nVial:            5 mg BPC-157 + 5 mg TB-500 (10 mg total)\n\nReconstitution:  Add 2 mL bacteriostatic water\n\nConcentration:   250 mcg of each peptide per 10 units on insulin syringe\n\nDose:            250 to 500 mcg of each peptide per day\n\nInjection:       10 to 20 units daily on insulin syringe\n\nFrequency:       Once daily\n\nTiming:          Any time of day (no fasting required)\n\nAdministration:  Subcutaneous injection\n\nDuration:        4 to 8 weeks for active healing\n\nBY APPLICATION\n\nAcute injury (strain, sprain, tear):        250 to 500 mcg daily for 4 to 6 weeks\n\nPost-surgical recovery:                     250 to 500 mcg daily for 4 to 8 weeks (with surgeon approval)\n\nChronic injury or joint support:            250 to 500 mcg daily for 6 to 8 weeks\n\nMultiple simultaneous injuries:             500 mcg daily for 6 to 8 weeks\n\nBY BODY WEIGHT\n\nUnder 150 lbs:   250 mcg daily of each peptide\n\n150 to 200 lbs:  250 to 400 mcg daily of each peptide\n\nOver 200 lbs:    400 to 500 mcg daily of each peptide\n\nWhere to Inject\n\nThis is where the Wolverine Stack has a practical advantage. TB-500 works systemically regardless of where you inject it. BPC-157 concentrates near the injection site due to its short half-life. …","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":250,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.25}],"strengths":[5,10,15,20,30]},{"name":"Cagrilintide","protocolName":"Cagrilintide","category":"Weight Loss / Metabolic","overview":"Cagrilintide is a long acting amylin analog that works through a completely different pathway than GLP-1 drugs like semaglutide, tirzepatide, and retatrutide. While those drugs have dominated the weight loss conversation, cagrilintide offers something none of them can: appetite suppression through a second, independent system in your brain that GLP-1 compounds do not touch.\n\nAmylin is a hormone your pancreas releases alongside insulin every time you eat. Its job is to tell your brain you are full. The problem is that natural amylin breaks down in your body within minutes. It does not last long enough to make a sustained impact. Cagrilintide is engineered to last 7 to 8 days, which is why you only need to inject once per week. Think of it as a louder, cleaner version of a signal your body already produces.\n\nThe real power of cagrilintide is in combination therapy. In the REDEFINE 1 trial, cagrilintide paired with semaglutide (called CagriSema) produced over 20% average weight loss, with 60% of participants losing 20% or more of their body weight and 23% losing 30% or more. Those are among the most significant results seen with any anti-obesity medication to date.\n\nWhile the clinical trials studied cagrilintide combined with semaglutide, the mechanism applies to any GLP-1 type compound. Pairing cagrilintide with retatrutide, tirzepatide, or semaglutide adds a full strength appetite signal through the amylin pathway that none of those compounds activate on their own. This makes cagrilintide a primary strategy for anyone using a GLP-1 type peptide who still experiences significant hunger or food noise.\n\nCagrilintide is currently in Phase 3 clinical trials. It is not yet FDA approved but is available through research peptide suppliers.","schedule":"titration schedule similar to other incretin therapies. This protocol is based on the Phase 3 REDEFINE trial design.\n\nSubcutaneous injection once weekly.\n\nWeeks       Dose\n1 to 4      0.25 mg (250 mcg)\n5 to 8      0.5 mg\n9 to 12     1.0 mg\n13 to 16    1.7 mg\n17+         2.4 mg (maintenance)\n\nIf Combining With Semaglutide, Tirzepatide, or Retatrutide:\n\nInject cagrilintide separately from your GLP-1 compound. You can inject on the same day but use different injection sites. Cagrilintide requires a different pH and is not compatible with other peptides in the same vial.\n\nIf you are starting both compounds at the same time, titrate both using their respective schedules. If you are adding cagrilintide to an existing GLP-1 protocol, start cagrilintide at 0.25 mg and follow the titration above while continuing your current GLP-1 dose.\n\nImportant Notes:\n\nThe slow titration reduces nausea and GI side effects. Do not rush it.\n\nSome people get good results at lower doses. You do not have to push to 2.4 mg if a lower dose is working and you are tolerating it well.\n\nIf side effects are strong at any step, stay at that dose for another 4 weeks before moving up.\n\nTake your injection on the same day each week.\n\nEat smaller meals during the titration phase. Avoid greasy, fried, or spicy foods. Ginger tea helps with nausea if you experience it.\n\nThe Protein Warning\nThis section exists because strong appetite suppression creates a problem most people do not see coming.\n\nWhen cagrilintide is combined with a GLP-1 compound, appetite suppression gets very strong. The scale moves fast. You think everything is working. But appetite suppression makes it very easy to undereat. And if you undereat protein, you lose muscle with the fat. Your body does not care. It will break down muscle for energy if you do not give it protein.\n\nHere is what happens when protein is not prioritized:\n\nWeeks 1 to 4, the scale moves fast and you think you are crushing it. Around months 2 to 3, your strength starts dropping. Your clothes fit weird. Something feels off. Long term, you end up skinny fat. Your metabolism is slower. You regain weight easier. …","frequency":"Weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":0.25,"unit":"mg","mg":0.25}],"strengths":[2,5,10,20]},{"name":"Cagrilintide + Semaglutide","protocolName":"Semaglutide","category":"Weight Loss / Metabolic","overview":"Semaglutide is a GLP-1 receptor agonist, which means it mimics a hormone your gut already produces called glucagon-like peptide-1. Your body naturally releases GLP-1 from cells in your small intestine after you eat, and it plays a role in blood sugar regulation, appetite signaling, and gastric motility. Semaglutide is a synthetic version of that hormone, engineered to last much longer in your body so it can be dosed once per week.\n\nIt was first approved by the FDA in 2017 under the brand name Ozempic for type 2 diabetes management at doses up to 2.0 mg per week. In 2021, the FDA approved a higher-dose version at 2.4 mg per week under the brand name Wegovy for chronic weight management. In December 2025, the FDA approved an oral tablet version of Wegovy at 25 mg daily for weight management, making it the first oral GLP-1 agonist approved for obesity.\n\nSemaglutide has been studied in some of the largest and most rigorous clinical trials in obesity medicine. The STEP program includes multiple Phase 3 trials enrolling thousands of participants, and the SELECT cardiovascular outcomes trial enrolled over 17,000 people. This is a drug with an enormous amount of published human data behind it.\n\nThat said, semaglutide is a single-receptor agonist. It activates one receptor: GLP-1. Newer compounds like tirzepatide activate two receptors (GLP-1 and GIP), and retatrutide activates three (GLP-1, GIP, and glucagon). Understanding what semaglutide does and what it does not do at the receptor level is essential for understanding where it fits in the landscape of GLP-1 based therapies.","schedule":"STANDARD TITRATION (subcutaneous, weekly)\nWeeks 1 to 4:       0.25 mg (starting dose)\nWeeks 5 to 8:       0.5 mg\nWeeks 9 to 12:      1.0 mg\nWeeks 13 to 16:     1.7 mg\nWeek 17 onward:     2.4 mg (full weight loss maintenance dose)\n\nFOR TYPE 2 DIABETES (Ozempic)\nWeeks 1 to 4:       0.25 mg\nWeek 5 onward:      0.5 mg (can increase to 1.0 or 2.0 mg based on response)\n\nNot Everyone Needs the Maximum Dose\nThe 2.4 mg target is based on clinical trial protocols designed to show maximum effect. In practice, many people achieve excellent results at lower doses. Some people lose significant weight at 1.0 mg with minimal side effects. Pushing to 2.4 mg may add more side effects without proportional additional benefit. The optimal dose depends on how much weight you need to lose, how sensitive you are to the appetite suppression effects, how well you tolerate the gastrointestinal side effects, and whether you are using it primarily for metabolic health or for significant weight loss.\n\nImportant Notes\nDo not skip titration steps. Starting at higher doses causes severe nausea and vomiting. If side effects are strong at any step, stay at that dose for an additional 4 weeks before increasing. Take your injection on the same day each week. You can change the day as long as your last dose was at least 2 days prior.\n\nRECONSTITUTION (research grade lyophilized powder)\nVial size:      Varies (5 mg, 10 mg, 20 mg, 30 mg)\nBac water:      Adjust volume to achieve desired concentration\nStorage:        Refrigerate after reconstitution","frequency":"Weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":0.25,"unit":"mg","mg":0.25},{"value":0.5,"unit":"mg","mg":0.5},{"value":1,"unit":"mg","mg":1},{"value":1.7,"unit":"mg","mg":1.7},{"value":2.4,"unit":"mg","mg":2.4}],"strengths":[2.5,5,10,20]},{"name":"Cartalax","protocolName":"Cartalax","category":"Recovery / Repair","overview":"Cartalax is a synthetic tripeptide made up of three amino acids: alanine, glutamic acid, and aspartic acid. The amino acid sequence is written as Ala-Glu-Asp, which is why you will also see it called \"AED peptide\" or \"T-31.\" It belongs to the Khavinson peptide family of bioregulators developed at the St. Petersburg Institute of Bioregulation and Gerontology in Russia.\n\nCartalax is classified as a cytogen, which is a type of short synthetic peptide designed to target specific tissues and restore normal cellular function. While the name suggests a focus on cartilage (and it is often marketed for joint health), Cartalax actually works more broadly on fibroblasts. Fibroblasts are found throughout the body in cartilage, skin, blood vessels, kidneys, and other connective tissues.\n\nWhat makes Cartalax unusual is its size. At just three amino acids, it is one of the smallest bioactive peptides studied for tissue regeneration. Most peptides work by binding to receptors on the outside of cells. Cartalax appears to bypass that entirely. Its tiny structure allows it to penetrate cell membranes and even enter the nucleus, where it may interact directly with DNA to influence gene expression.\n\nCartalax was developed as part of Vladimir Khavinson's decades-long research program into peptide bioregulators for aging and tissue repair. Khavinson (1946 to 2024) served as Director of the St. Petersburg Institute of Bioregulation and Gerontology and Vice-President of the Gerontological Society of the Russian Academy of Sciences. The theory behind these compounds is that short peptides derived from healthy tissue can help restore function to aging or damaged cells by normalizing gene expression patterns.\n\nCartalax is not FDA approved. …","schedule":"Cartalax dosing follows the standard bioregulator peptide approach: short cycles repeated several times per year.\n\nUnderstanding Bioregulator Dosing\n\nCartalax is a tripeptide that works through gene expression and epigenetic mechanisms rather than receptor binding. These pathways require smaller amounts to influence cellular behavior. The peptide's ability to penetrate cell nuclei means low doses can have meaningful effects in theory. The standard approach uses short intensive cycles rather than continuous daily use. This allows the epigenetic effects to consolidate during the off period.\n\nNo published dose-finding studies exist for Cartalax in humans. The protocols below come from the Khavinson research framework and community practice.\n\nSTANDARD INJECTABLE PROTOCOL\n\nRoute:          Subcutaneous injection\n\nDose:           2 mg once daily\n\nCycle length:   10 to 20 days\n\nFrequency:      Repeat cycles 3 to 4 times per year\n\nBreak:          3 to 4 months between cycles\n\nRECONSTITUTION\n\nVial size:      20 mg\n\nBac water:      2 mL\n\nConcentration:  10 mg/mL (10,000 mcg/mL)\n\nFor a 2 mg dose: 20 units on insulin syringe\n\nTiming Considerations\n\nMorning administration is generally preferred. Consistent timing helps establish effects. Can be taken with or without food. No fasting requirement.\n\nImportant Notes\n\nStart at the standard dose rather than titrating up. Effects are subtle and build over the cycle. This is not a stimulant and will not produce immediate noticeable effects. Benefits may continue after completing a cycle due to the epigenetic mechanism of action. Short cycles repeated throughout the year are preferred over continuous use.","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":2,"unit":"mg","mg":2}],"strengths":[20]},{"name":"CJC-1295 No DAC","protocolName":"CJC-1295","category":"Growth Hormone Family","overview":"CJC-1295 is a synthetic peptide that mimics growth hormone releasing hormone, the signal your hypothalamus naturally sends to your pituitary gland to tell it to produce and release growth hormone. Your body already makes this hormone. CJC-1295 is a lab-built version designed to do the same job but last much longer in your body than the natural version.\n\nThere are two versions of this peptide, and understanding the difference is important because they behave very differently in your body despite sharing the same core mechanism.\n\nCJC-1295 without DAC is also called Mod GRF 1-29, which stands for Modified Growth Hormone Releasing Factor (amino acids 1 through 29). Researchers discovered that only the first 29 amino acids of the full 44-amino-acid GHRH molecule are needed to activate the receptor on your pituitary gland. So they took those 29 amino acids and swapped out four of them at positions 2, 8, 15, and 27 for amino acids that resist enzymatic breakdown. The substitution at position 2 specifically blocks an enzyme called DPP-4 from cutting the peptide apart. The changes at positions 8 and 27 prevent chemical degradation through deamidation and oxidation. And the modification at position 15 enhances overall bioactivity. The result is a peptide that is far more stable than the natural hormone or its predecessor Sermorelin, but still clears your system relatively quickly with a half-life of about 30 minutes. That short half-life means it creates a sharp pulse of growth hormone release that closely mimics what your body does naturally, then clears out and lets your levels return to baseline. This is the most popular GHRH analog in this category and the standard choice for stacking with a ghrelin agonist like Ipamorelin. …","schedule":"practical protocols below represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus, not published clinical evidence.\n\nCJC-1295 without DAC (Mod GRF 1-29)\nThe short half-life requires daily dosing and careful timing around food intake.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\nDose:           100 to 300 mcg per injection\nFrequency:      Once daily (some protocols use 2 to 3 times daily)\nTiming:         Fasted, at least 2 hours after eating\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection\nDuration:       12 to 16 weeks continuous, then 4 weeks off\nSchedule:       Daily\n\nBY GOAL\nFat loss focus:    Inject before bed, fasted. Growth hormone sits in your system\novernight and drives fat burning while you sleep.\nMuscle growth:     Inject in the morning, 30 minutes before your first meal.\nThe meal provides the insulin your liver needs to convert\ngrowth hormone into IGF-1 for muscle protein synthesis.\nGeneral optimization: Nighttime dosing is most common because it amplifies\nyour natural overnight GH pulse.\n\nCJC-1295 without DAC is almost always combined with Ipamorelin. The two compounds are dosed together in the same injection at a 1 to 1 ratio. A common starting protocol is 100 mcg of each, taken together once nightly on an empty stomach. Some people scale up to 200 to 300 mcg of each depending on response and IGF-1 levels.\n\nCJC-1295 with DAC\nThe long half-life allows for infrequent dosing.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\nDose:           1 to 2 mg per week\nFrequency:      Once or twice weekly\nTiming:         No strict timing requirements due to long half-life\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection\nDuration:       8 to 12 weeks\n\nSome users split a weekly dose into two injections (for example, 1 mg on Sunday and 1 mg on Wednesday) to maintain more consistent levels, though with a half-life of 6 to 8 days this is generally not necessary.\n\nImportant Timing Considerations\nFor CJC-1295 without DAC, food timing is critical. …","frequency":"Twice weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":100,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.1},{"value":1,"unit":"mg","mg":1}],"strengths":[2,5,10]},{"name":"CJC-1295 No DAC + Ipamorelin","protocolName":"CJC-1295 no DAC / Ipamorelin Blend","category":"Growth Hormone Family","overview":"CJC-1295 + Ipamorelin is a combination of two growth hormone secretagogue peptides that work through different pathways to increase your body's natural growth hormone production. This blend has become the standard entry point for growth hormone peptide therapy because it combines the two cleanest compounds in each of the two major growth hormone signaling categories.\n\nCJC-1295 is a synthetic analog of growth hormone releasing hormone (GHRH), which is a signal your hypothalamus naturally sends to your pituitary gland to tell it to produce and release growth hormone. Think of CJC-1295 as building production capacity because you are telling the factory to make more product.\n\nIpamorelin is a synthetic ghrelin mimetic, meaning it mimics the hormone ghrelin that your gut produces. Ghrelin also signals your pituitary gland to release growth hormone, but through a completely separate receptor than GHRH. Ghrelin triggers immediate release of stored growth hormone and simultaneously suppresses somatostatin, which is the brake on the growth hormone system. Think of ipamorelin as emptying the warehouse because it triggers release of what has already been made and removes the inhibition at the same time.\n\nThe reason these two are blended together rather than used separately is synergy. When both pathways fire simultaneously, the growth hormone response is far greater than adding the individual effects together. Research shows this synergistic combination stimulates significantly more growth hormone release than either compound alone, because GHRH increases the number of pituitary cells releasing growth hormone while ghrelin agonists increase the amount released per cell.\n\nThe version of CJC-1295 used in this blend is CJC-1295 without DAC (also called Mod GRF 1-29). …","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\nCJC-1295 (no DAC):  100 to 300 mcg per day\nIpamorelin:         100 to 300 mcg per day\nTiming:             Once daily, fasted (at least 2 hours after eating)\nPreferred timing:   Before bed on an empty stomach\nSchedule:           Daily\nCycle length:       12 to 16 weeks, then 4 weeks off\n\nBY BODY WEIGHT\nUnder 150 lbs:  100 to 150 mcg of each peptide\n150 to 200 lbs: 200 mcg of each peptide\n200 to 250 lbs: 250 to 300 mcg of each peptide\nOver 250 lbs:   300 mcg of each peptide\n\nSPLIT DOSING (ADVANCED)\nMorning (fasted):  100 to 150 mcg of each\nBefore bed:        100 to 150 mcg of each\nThis creates two separate growth hormone pulses per day.\n\nRECONSTITUTION (standard blend vial)\nVial size:      10 mg total (5 mg CJC-1295 no DAC + 5 mg Ipamorelin)\nBac water:      2 mL\nConcentration:  250 mcg of each peptide per 10 units on insulin syringe\nAt 250 mcg of each per day, one vial lasts approximately 20 days.\n\nWhy Fasting Matters\nWhen you eat, your blood sugar rises and your body releases insulin. Insulin binds directly to the cells in your pituitary gland that produce growth hormone and suppresses their ability to release it. If you inject these peptides while insulin is present in your blood, you are pressing the gas and the brake at the same time. Take the injection at least 2 hours after eating to ensure insulin has cleared.\n\nTiming Based on Your Goal\nOnce growth hormone is released, it travels to your liver where it gets converted into IGF-1. But your liver needs insulin present to efficiently convert growth hormone into IGF-1. This creates a timing consideration.\n\nIf your primary goal is fat loss, injecting before bed makes the most sense. …","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":100,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.1},{"value":250,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.25}],"strengths":[5,10,20]},{"name":"CJC-1295 With DAC","protocolName":"CJC-1295 with DAC","category":"Growth Hormone Family","overview":"CJC-1295 is a synthetic peptide that mimics growth hormone releasing hormone, the signal your hypothalamus naturally sends to your pituitary gland to tell it to produce and release growth hormone. Your body already makes this hormone. CJC-1295 is a lab-built version designed to do the same job but last much longer in your body than the natural version.\n\nThere are two versions of this peptide, and understanding the difference is important because they behave very differently in your body despite sharing the same core mechanism.\n\nCJC-1295 without DAC is also called Mod GRF 1-29, which stands for Modified Growth Hormone Releasing Factor (amino acids 1 through 29). Researchers discovered that only the first 29 amino acids of the full 44-amino-acid GHRH molecule are needed to activate the receptor on your pituitary gland. So they took those 29 amino acids and swapped out four of them at positions 2, 8, 15, and 27 for amino acids that resist enzymatic breakdown. The substitution at position 2 specifically blocks an enzyme called DPP-4 from cutting the peptide apart. The changes at positions 8 and 27 prevent chemical degradation through deamidation and oxidation. And the modification at position 15 enhances overall bioactivity. The result is a peptide that is far more stable than the natural hormone or its predecessor Sermorelin, but still clears your system relatively quickly with a half-life of about 30 minutes. That short half-life means it creates a sharp pulse of growth hormone release that closely mimics what your body does naturally, then clears out and lets your levels return to baseline. This is the most popular GHRH analog in this category and the standard choice for stacking with a ghrelin agonist like Ipamorelin. …","schedule":"practical protocols below represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus, not published clinical evidence.\n\nCJC-1295 without DAC (Mod GRF 1-29)\nThe short half-life requires daily dosing and careful timing around food intake.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\nDose:           100 to 300 mcg per injection\nFrequency:      Once daily (some protocols use 2 to 3 times daily)\nTiming:         Fasted, at least 2 hours after eating\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection\nDuration:       12 to 16 weeks continuous, then 4 weeks off\nSchedule:       Daily\n\nBY GOAL\nFat loss focus:    Inject before bed, fasted. Growth hormone sits in your system\novernight and drives fat burning while you sleep.\nMuscle growth:     Inject in the morning, 30 minutes before your first meal.\nThe meal provides the insulin your liver needs to convert\ngrowth hormone into IGF-1 for muscle protein synthesis.\nGeneral optimization: Nighttime dosing is most common because it amplifies\nyour natural overnight GH pulse.\n\nCJC-1295 without DAC is almost always combined with Ipamorelin. The two compounds are dosed together in the same injection at a 1 to 1 ratio. A common starting protocol is 100 mcg of each, taken together once nightly on an empty stomach. Some people scale up to 200 to 300 mcg of each depending on response and IGF-1 levels.\n\nCJC-1295 with DAC\nThe long half-life allows for infrequent dosing.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\nDose:           1 to 2 mg per week\nFrequency:      Once or twice weekly\nTiming:         No strict timing requirements due to long half-life\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection\nDuration:       8 to 12 weeks\n\nSome users split a weekly dose into two injections (for example, 1 mg on Sunday and 1 mg on Wednesday) to maintain more consistent levels, though with a half-life of 6 to 8 days this is generally not necessary.\n\nImportant Timing Considerations\nFor CJC-1295 without DAC, food timing is critical. …","frequency":"Twice weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":100,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.1},{"value":1,"unit":"mg","mg":1}],"strengths":[2,5,10]},{"name":"Dihexa","protocolName":"Dihexa","category":"Cognitive / Nootropic / Sleep","overview":"Dihexa (N-hexanoic-Tyr-Ile-(6) aminohexanoic amide) is a synthetic peptide developed by Joseph Harding and his research team at Washington State University. It is a modified derivative of angiotensin IV, which is part of the renin-angiotensin system. The compound was originally developed as a potential therapy for Alzheimer's disease and cognitive decline.\n\nDihexa is the most potent and least studied compound in the cognitive peptide space. Unlike nootropics that modulate neurotransmitter levels (like Semax and Selank do with BDNF and serotonin), dihexa works through a fundamentally different mechanism. The primary published research claims it promotes synaptogenesis, which is the actual formation of new synaptic connections between neurons. This means it is not just tweaking how your existing neural connections communicate. It is theoretically stimulating the creation of entirely new connections.\n\nThere are critical caveats with dihexa that need to be addressed upfront. The research is entirely preclinical. No human studies have been published. All cognitive data comes from animal models. And importantly, a foundational 2014 mechanism study from the Harding lab was formally retracted in April 2025 after a Washington State University investigation confirmed data fabrication in the western blot images. This retraction weakens the confidence in the proposed mechanism of action, though it does not necessarily invalidate the behavioral data from separate experiments.\n\nDihexa is not FDA approved. It is not available through compounding pharmacies as a prescription. It is available only as a research compound. It is also the peptide with the most significant gap between community enthusiasm and published evidence. I want to be very direct about that.","schedule":"Practical Protocols\n\nThe following protocols are entirely based on community practice. They are NOT derived from published research.\n\nORAL PROTOCOL (most common community approach)\n\nProtocol       Dose          Frequency    Duration\n\nConservative   5 to 10 mg    Daily        4 to 6 weeks\n\nStandard       10 to 20 mg   Daily        4 to 8 weeks\n\nHigher range   20 to 30 mg   Daily        4 to 8 weeks\n\nSUBCUTANEOUS PROTOCOL (less common)\n\nProtocol       Dose          Frequency    Duration\n\nConservative   5 to 10 mg    Daily        4 to 6 weeks\n\nStandard       10 to 20 mg   Daily        4 to 8 weeks\n\nTRANSDERMAL PROTOCOL (used by some practitioners)\n\nApplied to the inner forearms. Dose varies by preparation.\n\nOral administration is the most commonly used route because dihexa is orally bioavailable and crosses the blood-brain barrier, which is unusual for a peptide. This was demonstrated in the primary 2013 Harding lab rat study.\n\nWhy I Am Cautious About Dosing Recommendations\n\nI want to be transparent about something. With most peptides in this course, the dosing protocols are informed by at least some combination of animal dose-response data, clinical practice patterns, and community experience accumulated over years of use. With dihexa, the animal data has integrity concerns, there are zero human studies, the community experience is inconsistent, and the mechanism of action is uncertain. I am including the protocols people are using because you deserve to know what is out there, but I want to be clear that confidence in these doses is lower than for any other compound in this course.","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":5,"unit":"mg","mg":5},{"value":10,"unit":"mg","mg":10},{"value":20,"unit":"mg","mg":20}],"strengths":[10]},{"name":"DSIP","protocolName":"DSIP","category":"Cognitive / Nootropic / Sleep","overview":"DSIP stands for Delta Sleep Inducing Peptide. It is a naturally occurring peptide that promotes deep, restorative sleep. It was discovered in 1977 when Swiss researchers Schoenenberger and Monnier isolated it from the cerebral venous blood of rabbits during electrically induced slow wave sleep. They named it for its ability to induce delta brain waves, which are the signature of the deepest stage of sleep.\n\nUnlike sleeping pills that sedate you into unconsciousness, DSIP works by optimizing your natural sleep architecture. It promotes slow wave sleep, also called delta sleep, which is when your body does most of its physical repair, releases growth hormone, and consolidates memories. This is the stage of sleep that leaves you feeling actually rested.\n\nDSIP is a small peptide made of just nine amino acids with the sequence Trp-Ala-Gly-Gly-Asp-Ala-Ser-Gly-Glu. Your body produces it naturally in the hypothalamus, and it crosses the blood brain barrier easily, which allows it to directly influence brain function. Beyond sleep, DSIP also affects stress hormones, pain perception, and hormone release.\n\nWhat makes DSIP interesting is that it does not create dependency like benzodiazepines or other sleep medications. It does not knock you out and leave you groggy the next day. Instead, it appears to work with your body's natural rhythms to enhance sleep quality. However, there is an important caveat. The published research on DSIP is older, the results are mixed, and one controlled study concluded that short term DSIP treatment is \"not likely to be of major therapeutic benefit\" for chronic insomnia. This is a peptide where the mechanism is interesting, the clinical data is limited, and you need to set realistic expectations. …","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\nDose:           100 to 300 mcg per day\nFrequency:      Once daily\nTiming:         30 to 60 minutes before bed\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection\nDuration:       5 to 10 consecutive nights, then evaluate\n\nBY BODY WEIGHT\nUnder 150 lbs:  100 to 150 mcg nightly\n150 to 200 lbs: 150 to 250 mcg nightly\nOver 200 lbs:   200 to 300 mcg nightly\n\nBY APPLICATION\nGeneral sleep optimization:    100 to 200 mcg nightly\nStress related sleep issues:   200 to 300 mcg nightly\nGH peptide sleep disruption:   100 to 200 mcg nightly\nShift work sleep support:      200 to 300 mcg before target sleep time\n\nRECONSTITUTION (standard)\nVial size:      10 mg\nBac water:      2 mL\nConcentration:  500 mcg per 10 units on insulin syringe\nFor 200 mcg:    4 units on insulin syringe\n\nCycling\nThere is no established cycling protocol from published research. Based on community experience and the theoretical concern about receptor adaptation, common approaches include using it for 5 to 10 consecutive nights then taking a break, or using it 3 to 5 nights per week rather than every night. If you find it stops working after prolonged daily use, take a 1 to 2 week break.\n\nTiming\nAdminister 30 to 60 minutes before your target sleep time. Earlier timing (2 to 3 hours before bed) has been reported as less effective by some users. The onset is relatively quick at 10 to 30 minutes.","frequency":"Weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":100,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.1}],"strengths":[2,5,10,15]},{"name":"FOXO4-DRI","protocolName":"FOXO4-DRI","category":"Mitochondrial / Longevity","overview":"FOXO4-DRI is a synthetic peptide designed to selectively eliminate senescent cells, which are damaged or aged cells that have stopped dividing but refuse to die. These cells accumulate in your tissues over time and pump out inflammatory signals that damage the healthy cells around them. Scientists sometimes call them zombie cells.\n\nThe peptide was developed by Dr. Peter de Keizer and colleagues at Erasmus University Medical Center in the Netherlands. Their landmark 2017 study published in Cell demonstrated that FOXO4-DRI could selectively kill senescent cells in mice without harming healthy cells, leading to measurable improvements in fitness, fur density, and kidney function.\n\nThe name comes from its mechanism. It is a D-Retro-Inverso (DRI) modified version of a portion of the FOXO4 protein. The DRI modification means the normal L-amino acids have been replaced with D-amino acids in a reversed sequence. This makes the peptide resistant to normal enzymatic breakdown, allowing it to remain active in the body much longer than a standard peptide. Where most peptides get chewed up within minutes, FOXO4-DRI can remain stable for 72 hours or more after administration.\n\nFOXO4-DRI belongs to a class of compounds called senolytics, which specifically target and eliminate senescent cells. Other senolytics include the drug combination of dasatinib plus quercetin, and BCL-2 inhibitors like ABT-263. What makes FOXO4-DRI unique is its selectivity. In the original research, it showed an 11.73 fold preference for killing senescent cells versus healthy cells.\n\nThis is a research peptide with no completed human clinical trials. Cleara Biotech, a company formed to commercialize the academic work on FOXO4-DRI, remains a preclinical concern as of 2025. …","schedule":"STANDARD PROTOCOL (Community Consensus)\nConservative:   2 to 3 mg subcutaneous every other day, 3 doses total\nStandard:       3 to 5 mg subcutaneous every other day, 3 doses total\nAggressive:     5 to 10 mg subcutaneous every other day, 3 doses total\n\nRepeat cycles:  1 to 3 times per year\n\nRECONSTITUTION\nVial size:      10 mg\nBac water:      1 mL\nConcentration:  10 mg/mL (100 mcg per unit on insulin syringe)\nFor a 5 mg dose: 50 units on insulin syringe\n\nALTERNATIVE PROTOCOL\nLow-dose daily: 0.3 to 0.5 mg daily for 7 days\nRepeat:         Every other week\nRationale:      Consistent low-level disruption of FOXO4-p53 interaction\nNote:           Less precedent than the standard cycling approach\n\nWhy the Short Cycles\nSenescent cell clearance is not an ongoing process requiring continuous treatment. The goal is to eliminate accumulated senescent cells, then allow healthy tissue to regenerate. Repeating treatment 1 to 3 times per year addresses new senescent cells that accumulate over time without maintaining constant peptide exposure.\n\nLong rest periods between treatments serve multiple purposes. …","frequency":"Every other day","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":2,"unit":"mg","mg":2},{"value":3,"unit":"mg","mg":3},{"value":5,"unit":"mg","mg":5},{"value":0.3,"unit":"mg","mg":0.3}],"strengths":[2,5,10,16]},{"name":"GHK-Cu","protocolName":"GHK-Cu","category":"Recovery / Repair","overview":"GHK-Cu stands for glycyl-L-histidyl-L-lysine copper. It is a naturally occurring copper peptide complex made of three amino acids, glycine, histidine, and lysine, bound to a copper ion. Your body already produces this compound. You can find it in your blood plasma, your saliva, and your urine.\n\nThink of GHK-Cu as your body's natural signal to start repairing damaged tissue. Whether that damage comes from aging, injury, sun exposure, or the stress you put on your body from training, when tissue gets damaged, your body releases GHK-Cu. It tells your cells to make new collagen, grow new blood vessels, and repair what has been broken. It is like your body's construction crew foreman, coordinating the entire rebuilding operation when something breaks.\n\nGHK was first isolated from human plasma albumin in 1973 by Loren Pickart. Researchers noticed that liver tissue from older patients behaved differently when incubated in blood from younger patients. The older liver cells started functioning more like younger cells. The active factor responsible for that effect was identified as GHK.\n\nHere is the problem. As you age, your GHK-Cu levels drop significantly. At 20 years old, you have about 200 nanograms per milliliter floating around in your plasma. By the time you hit 60, that number drops to around 80 nanograms per milliliter. More than half of it is gone. That decline shows up everywhere. Your skin loses elasticity. It gets thinner. Wounds heal slower. Recovery from training takes longer. Your hair starts to thin because your follicles shrink. Less GHK-Cu means your construction crew is understaffed. Repairs happen slower and you get lower quality results.\n\nGHK-Cu is widely used in cosmetic products, where it is listed as Copper Tripeptide-1 on ingredient labels. …","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nSTANDARD INJECTABLE PROTOCOL (Subcutaneous)\nDose:           1 to 2 mg per day\nFrequency:      Daily or 5 days per week\nDuration:       4 to 8 weeks (can extend to 12 weeks)\nBreak:          4 to 6 weeks between cycles\n\nCONSERVATIVE PROTOCOL (Starting Point)\nDose:           0.5 to 1 mg per day\nFrequency:      3 to 5 times per week\nDuration:       Start here to assess tolerance before increasing\n\nTOPICAL PROTOCOL\nConcentration:  0.5% to 4% (facial products), 2% (eye area)\nFrequency:      Once or twice daily\nDuration:       Continuous use, no cycling required\nTimeline:       8 to 16 weeks for skin effects, 16 to 24 weeks for hair\n\nRECONSTITUTION (standard for 50 mg vial)\nVial size:      50 mg\nBac water:      2.5 to 3 mL\nConcentration:  At 2.5 mL, 1 mg = 5 units on insulin syringe\nNote:           Solution will turn blue due to the copper. This is normal.\n\nAdministration Route Selection\nSubcutaneous injection is for systemic effects including wound healing, tissue regeneration, and general anti-aging benefits. It reaches deep tissues like muscles, tendons, ligaments, and organs that topical application will never touch. …","frequency":"Twice daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":1,"unit":"mg","mg":1},{"value":0.5,"unit":"mg","mg":0.5}],"strengths":[50,100]},{"name":"GHRP-2 Acetate","protocolName":"GHRP-2","category":"Growth Hormone Family","overview":"GHRP-2 stands for Growth Hormone Releasing Peptide 2. Its pharmaceutical name is Pralmorelin, and it is a synthetic hexapeptide that stimulates your pituitary gland to release growth hormone. It was one of the earliest growth hormone secretagogues developed and is actually approved in Japan as a diagnostic agent for growth hormone deficiency.\n\nGHRP-2 works by binding to ghrelin receptors in your pituitary gland and hypothalamus. Ghrelin is the hormone your gut produces to signal hunger and trigger growth hormone release. GHRP-2 mimics that signal. Unlike synthetic HGH injections, GHRP-2 stimulates your body's own production of growth hormone rather than introducing an external hormone. This means your natural feedback loop stays intact.\n\nThe peptide has been studied via intravenous, subcutaneous, intranasal, and oral administration routes. It has been used in clinical research for growth hormone deficiency in children, appetite stimulation studies, and diagnostic testing. It has also been studied in animals for tendon healing applications.\n\nGHRP-2 sits in the middle of the GHRP family in terms of selectivity. It produces a stronger growth hormone pulse than ipamorelin in some studies, but it also triggers mild increases in cortisol and prolactin that ipamorelin does not. It is cleaner than GHRP-6 and hexarelin, but not as selective as ipamorelin.","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nSTANDARD SOLO PROTOCOL\nDose:           100 to 300 mcg per injection\nFrequency:      2 to 3 times daily\nTiming:         Morning, pre-workout, and/or before bed (fasted)\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection\nSchedule:       Daily\nCycle length:   8 to 16 weeks\n\nCOMBINED WITH CJC-1295 (PREFERRED)\nGHRP-2:             100 to 200 mcg per injection\nCJC-1295 without DAC: 100 to 200 mcg per injection\nFrequency:           2 to 3 times daily\nSchedule:            Daily\nCycle length:        8 to 12 weeks\n\nThe combination produces significantly more growth hormone than either peptide alone. GHRP-2 triggers the pulse (empties the warehouse) while CJC-1295 amplifies and extends it (builds production capacity). Research shows the synergistic effect can produce 77 to 225% greater GH release than either compound alone.\n\nDOSING BY BODY WEIGHT\nUnder 150 lbs:  100 to 150 mcg per injection\n150 to 200 lbs: 150 to 200 mcg per injection\nOver 200 lbs:   200 to 300 mcg per injection\n\nTiming and Administration\nTake on an empty stomach, at least 1 hour before eating or 2 to 3 hours after eating. Food, especially carbohydrates, raises insulin and blunts the growth hormone response. Wait at least 30 minutes after injection before eating.\n\nThe fasting requirement exists because insulin and growth hormone have an inverse relationship. When insulin is elevated, your body suppresses growth hormone release. This is also why your biggest natural GH pulse happens during overnight sleep when you are fasted. …","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":100,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.1}],"strengths":[5,10,15]},{"name":"GHRP-6 Acetate","protocolName":"GHRP-6","category":"Growth Hormone Family","overview":"GHRP-6 stands for Growth Hormone Releasing Peptide 6. It is the original synthetic growth hormone secretagogue, developed in the 1980s by researcher Cyril Bowers at Tulane University. GHRP-6 was the first peptide to demonstrate that specific amino acid sequences could trigger powerful growth hormone release from the pituitary gland. This discovery established the foundation for every subsequent growth hormone releasing peptide, including GHRP-2, hexarelin, and ipamorelin.\n\nThe peptide consists of six amino acids with the sequence His-D-Trp-Ala-Trp-D-Phe-Lys-NH2. It was derived from met-enkephalin through computer modeling and structural modification. The inclusion of unnatural D-amino acids in the sequence gives GHRP-6 its unique properties and prevents it from having opioid activity.\n\nWhat sets GHRP-6 apart from every other GHRP is its strong appetite stimulation. When GHRP-6 binds to ghrelin receptors, it triggers both growth hormone release and intense hunger. This dual action makes it particularly useful for people who struggle to eat enough, such as during bulking phases or when recovering from illness. For most people outside those situations, the hunger and the broader side effect profile make it a poor first choice compared to more selective options like ipamorelin.\n\nGHRP-6 has been extensively studied in both animal models and humans. Safety studies have confirmed it is well tolerated intravenously in healthy volunteers and has no pharmacological interaction with cardiovascular medications like beta blockers. Beyond growth hormone release, GHRP-6 has been researched for cardioprotective properties, wound healing, and organ protection through its binding to a second receptor called CD36.","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nSTANDARD SOLO PROTOCOL\nDose:           100 to 300 mcg per injection\nFrequency:      2 to 3 times daily\nTiming:         Morning, pre-workout, and/or before bed (fasted)\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection\nSchedule:       Daily\nCycle length:   8 to 16 weeks\nCOMBINED WITH CJC-1295 (PREFERRED)\nGHRP-6:              100 to 200 mcg per injection\nCJC-1295 without DAC: 100 to 200 mcg per injection\nFrequency:            2 to 3 times daily\nSchedule:             Daily\nCycle length:         8 to 12 weeks\nThe combination produces significantly more growth hormone than either peptide alone due to the synergistic effect of hitting both the GHRH and ghrelin pathways simultaneously.\n\nDOSING BY BODY WEIGHT\nUnder 150 lbs:  100 to 150 mcg per injection\n150 to 200 lbs: 150 to 200 mcg per injection\nOver 200 lbs:   200 to 300 mcg per injection\nTiming and Administration\nTake on an empty stomach, at least 1 hour before eating or 2 to 3 hours after eating. Food, especially carbohydrates, raises insulin and blunts the growth hormone response. Wait at least 30 minutes after injection before eating.\n\nThe intense hunger typically hits 15 to 30 minutes after injection. If you are using the appetite effect strategically, time your injection 30 minutes before your largest meal so the hunger hits when you are ready to eat. This turns the side effect into a tool.\n\nCycling\nGHRP-6 is one of the more aggressive GHRPs with significant effects on cortisol, prolactin, and appetite. Research on hexarelin showed that chronic twice-daily use for 16 weeks led to partial attenuation of the GH response (reduced by about 45% from baseline), but the attenuation fully reversed after 4 weeks off. …","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":100,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.1}],"strengths":[5,10]},{"name":"Glutathione","protocolName":"Glutathione","category":"Antioxidant / Wellness","overview":"Glutathione is a tripeptide composed of three amino acids: glutamate, cysteine, and glycine. It is not a traditional peptide therapeutic. It is an endogenous compound, meaning your body already makes it naturally. Every cell in your body contains glutathione, and it is the most abundant intracellular antioxidant in the human body. It is often called the \"master antioxidant\" because of the central role it plays in neutralizing free radicals, supporting detoxification, recycling other antioxidants, and maintaining immune function.\n\nYour body synthesizes glutathione primarily in the liver. It exists in two forms: the active reduced form (GSH) and the inactive oxidized form (GSSG). When glutathione neutralizes a free radical, it donates an electron and becomes oxidized. The enzyme glutathione reductase then converts it back to the active reduced form so it can go to work again. A healthy cell maintains a ratio of roughly 90% GSH to 10% GSSG, and when that ratio shifts toward the oxidized side, it signals that the cell is under oxidative stress.\n\nGlutathione levels decline with age. A study in the American Journal of Clinical Nutrition measured red blood cell glutathione in elderly adults (60 to 75 years) versus younger adults (30 to 40 years) and found that older subjects had 46.2% lower glutathione concentrations. The decline is driven by reduced availability of precursor amino acids, particularly cysteine, rather than a failure of the synthetic enzymes themselves.\n\nBeyond aging, glutathione depletion is accelerated by chronic stress, illness, environmental toxin exposure, alcohol consumption, certain medications (particularly acetaminophen), and poor nutrition. …","schedule":"Practical Protocols (Injectable)\n\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns.\n\nSUBCUTANEOUS PROTOCOL\n\nConservative:   100 mg    3 times weekly    8 to 12 weeks\n\nStandard:       200 mg    3 times weekly    8 to 12 weeks\n\nIntensive:      200 mg    daily or 5x/wk    4 to 8 weeks\n\nWeekly totals typically range from 300 to 1000 mg for subcutaneous protocols.\n\nINTRAMUSCULAR PROTOCOL\n\nStarting:       200 mg        weekly    4 weeks\n\nMaintenance:    400 to 600 mg weekly    ongoing\n\nMaximum:        800 to 1000 mg weekly   as needed\n\nStart low and increase every 4 weeks based on tolerance and response.\n\nPractical Protocols (Oral)\n\nORAL (LIPOSOMAL)\n\nStandard:       500 mg daily       ongoing\n\nHigh dose:      1000 mg daily      ongoing\n\nLiposomal formulations have significantly better absorption than conventional oral glutathione capsules based on published pharmacokinetic data.\n\nORAL (NAC AS PRECURSOR)\n\nStandard:       600 mg daily       ongoing\n\nHigh dose:      600 mg twice daily ongoing\n\nNAC provides cysteine, the rate-limiting amino acid for glutathione synthesis. …","frequency":"Twice daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":300,"unit":"mg","mg":300},{"value":400,"unit":"mg","mg":400},{"value":800,"unit":"mg","mg":800}],"strengths":[600,1500]},{"name":"Gonadorelin","protocolName":"Gonadorelin","category":"Hormone / Fertility","overview":"Gonadorelin is a synthetic version of GnRH (gonadotropin-releasing hormone), which is the hormone your hypothalamus naturally produces to signal your pituitary gland to release LH and FSH. It is a 10 amino acid peptide that is structurally identical to the natural GnRH your body makes.\n\nTo understand why gonadorelin matters, you have to understand the HPG axis. Your hypothalamus releases GnRH in pulses. Those pulses travel to your anterior pituitary gland and tell it to release LH (luteinizing hormone) and FSH (follicle-stimulating hormone). LH travels to the Leydig cells in your testes and stimulates testosterone production. FSH travels to the Sertoli cells and stimulates sperm production. This pulsatile signaling system is how your body regulates testosterone and fertility.\n\nGonadorelin is essentially giving your pituitary gland the same signal your hypothalamus would give it. The critical word here is pulsatile. Your natural GnRH is released in pulses roughly every 60 to 120 minutes. This pulsatile pattern is what keeps the pituitary responsive. If GnRH were released continuously instead of in pulses, the pituitary receptors would actually desensitize and stop responding. This is the basis of an entire class of drugs called GnRH agonists (like leuprolide) used to suppress testosterone in prostate cancer. They give continuous GnRH stimulation, which paradoxically shuts down LH and FSH release. Pulsatile stimulation maintains production. Continuous stimulation suppresses it. The dosing pattern determines the effect.\n\nGonadorelin became clinically relevant in the TRT world after the FDA reclassified HCG as a biologic in 2020, which made it unavailable from compounding pharmacies. …","schedule":"Practical Protocols\n\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns.\n\nSTANDARD TRT ADJUNCT PROTOCOL\n\nDose:           100 to 200 mcg subcutaneously\n\nFrequency:      Once or twice daily\n\nTiming:         Morning, or morning and evening\n\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection (abdomen or thigh)\n\nDuration:       Continuous alongside TRT\n\nTWICE-DAILY PROTOCOL (preferred for mimicking pulsatile release)\n\nMorning:        100 mcg subcutaneously\n\nEvening:        100 mcg subcutaneously\n\nRationale:      Two daily pulses better approximate natural GnRH\n\npulsatility than a single daily dose\n\nWHAT TO MONITOR ON LABS\n\nLH:             Confirm gonadorelin is stimulating pituitary response\n\nFSH:            Confirm FSH is being produced (important for fertility)\n\nTestosterone:   Monitor intratesticular production (if measurable)\n\nSperm analysis: If fertility is the goal\n\nTesticular size: Clinical assessment at follow-up visits\n\nRECONSTITUTION (standard)\n\nVial size:      2 mg\n\nBac water:      2 mL\n\nConcentration:  100 mcg per 10 units on insulin syringe\n\n100 mcg = 10 units\n\n200 mcg = 20 units","frequency":"Twice daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":100,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.1}],"strengths":[2,5]},{"name":"Hexarelin Acetate","protocolName":"Hexarelin","category":"Growth Hormone Family","overview":"Hexarelin is a synthetic growth hormone releasing peptide and one of the most potent compounds in the GHRP family. It stimulates your pituitary gland to release growth hormone by binding to ghrelin receptors, the same pathway that ipamorelin and GHRP-6 use. Unlike synthetic HGH injections, hexarelin triggers your body to produce its own growth hormone rather than introducing an external hormone.\n\nWhat sets hexarelin apart from other peptides in this class is raw potency. On a dose-for-dose basis, hexarelin produces the strongest growth hormone release of any GHRP available. A single injection can produce growth hormone spikes comparable to much higher doses of other peptides. But that potency comes with trade-offs. Unlike ipamorelin, hexarelin is not selective. It also raises cortisol and prolactin, which means the side effect profile is broader. It also desensitizes the ghrelin receptor faster than other compounds in this class, which means cycling is not optional. It is mandatory.\n\nThis makes hexarelin a purpose-built tool rather than a daily driver. If you need maximum growth hormone output for a specific reason and a defined time period, hexarelin delivers. For long-term optimization where you want a clean side effect profile and the ability to run the peptide for months, ipamorelin is the better choice.\n\nWhat also makes hexarelin unique is its cardiac effects. Research has identified a separate receptor for hexarelin in heart tissue, different from the ghrelin receptor in the pituitary. Studies show that hexarelin has direct cardioprotective properties that appear to be independent of its growth hormone releasing effects. This is not something any other GHRP has demonstrated to the same degree.\n\nHexarelin is a hexapeptide, meaning it contains six amino acids. …","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\nDose:       100 to 200 mcg per injection\nFrequency:  2 to 3 times daily\nTiming:     Morning (fasted) and before bed (fasted)\nSchedule:   4 to 8 weeks on, then 4 weeks off\n\nBEGINNER PROTOCOL\nWeeks 1 to 2:  100 mcg twice daily (morning and before bed)\nWeeks 3 to 4:  150 mcg twice daily\nWeeks 5 to 8:  200 mcg twice daily (or three times daily if tolerated)\nAfter cycle:   4 to 6 weeks off before repeating\n\nBY BODY WEIGHT\nUnder 150 lbs:  100 mcg per injection\n150 to 200 lbs: 100 to 150 mcg per injection\nOver 200 lbs:   150 to 200 mcg per injection\n\nRECONSTITUTION (standard)\nVial size:     5 mg\nBac water:     2 mL\nConcentration: 250 mcg per 10 units on insulin syringe\n\nImportant Notes\nTake on an empty stomach. At least 2 hours after eating, 30 minutes before eating. Food, especially carbohydrates, raises insulin and blunts the growth hormone response.\n\nCycling is mandatory. Hexarelin desensitizes the ghrelin receptor faster than ipamorelin. Research shows progressive decline in growth hormone response starting as early as week 1, with significant blunting by week 16. The standard practice is 4 to 8 weeks on, then 4 weeks off to allow full receptor recovery.\n\nMonitor for cortisol and prolactin effects. If you experience persistent anxiety, irritability, sleep disruption, or libido changes, reduce the dose or shorten the cycle.","frequency":"Twice daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":100,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.1}],"strengths":[2,5,10]},{"name":"Humanin","protocolName":"Humanin","category":"Mitochondrial / Longevity","overview":"Humanin is a 24-amino acid peptide encoded by a short open reading frame within the 16S ribosomal RNA region of mitochondrial DNA. Unlike most peptides used in the optimization space, humanin is not synthetic. Your body actually makes it. Every cell with functioning mitochondria can produce humanin, though levels decline significantly as you age.\n\nIt was first discovered in 2001 by Hashimoto and colleagues, who isolated it from the brain of an Alzheimer's disease patient. They found that this small peptide could rescue neuronal cells from death caused by amyloid-beta toxicity. Since then, humanin has been studied for neuroprotection, cardioprotection, insulin sensitivity, metabolic regulation, and longevity.\n\nHumanin belongs to a class of signaling molecules called mitochondrial-derived peptides (MDPs). Your mitochondria are not just energy factories. They also contain their own small genome, and that genome encodes several peptides that function as signaling molecules throughout the body. The other major MDPs are MOTS-C (which I have covered separately) and the small humanin-like peptides (SHLPs). These peptides appear to coordinate stress responses and metabolic regulation between your mitochondria and the rest of your cells.\n\nWhat makes humanin particularly interesting from a longevity perspective is the centenarian connection. Research from Pinchas Cohen's lab at USC and Nir Barzilai's group at Albert Einstein College of Medicine found that the children of centenarians, who are more likely to become centenarians themselves, have circulating humanin levels up to three times higher than age-matched controls. Meanwhile, humanin levels decline steadily with age in most people. …","schedule":"Humanin dosing in humans is not established through clinical trials. The protocols below are extrapolated from animal studies and extremely limited community experience.\n\nUnderstanding the Dose Context\nAnimal studies have used humanin and its analogs at doses ranging from 0.16 mcg/kg/min (central infusion) to 2 mg/kg (intraperitoneal bolus). These do not translate directly to human subcutaneous dosing. The short half-life means any single dose clears rapidly, which is likely why daily or twice-daily administration is used in practice.\n\nThe HNG analog (S14G-humanin) is approximately 1,000 times more potent than native humanin. Most research and community protocols use HNG rather than native humanin.\n\nNo published dose-finding studies exist for subcutaneous humanin administration in humans.\n\nCOMMUNITY PROTOCOL (Limited Precedent)\nLow dose:       25 to 50 mcg daily, subcutaneous\nStandard dose:  500 mcg to 1 mg once or twice daily, subcutaneous\nCycle length:   10 to 15 days\nFrequency:      Cycles repeated several times per year\n\nRECONSTITUTION (typical)\nVial size:      10 mg\nBac water:      2 mL\nConcentration:  5 mg/mL (5,000 mcg/mL)\nFor a 1 mg dose: 20 units on insulin syringe\n\nTiming Considerations\nMorning administration is commonly used. No published data on optimal timing. No fasting requirement. Can be administered at any time of day.\n\nImportant Notes\nThe short half-life is a significant limitation. Twice-daily dosing may provide more sustained exposure than once-daily. The short half-life likely explains why animal studies showed healthspan improvement without lifespan extension. More stable analogs or alternative delivery systems are active areas of research but not available for practical use.","frequency":"Twice daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":25,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.025}],"strengths":[10]},{"name":"IGF-1 LR3","protocolName":"IGF-1 LR3","category":"IGF / Growth","overview":"IGF-1 LR3 stands for Long Arginine 3 Insulin-like Growth Factor 1. It is a synthetic, modified version of the naturally occurring hormone IGF-1. The full peptide is 83 amino acids long, compared to the 70 amino acids in native IGF-1.\n\nTwo specific modifications set it apart from the natural hormone. First, the amino acid at position 3 has been swapped from glutamic acid to arginine. Second, 13 additional amino acids have been added to the N-terminus. These structural changes may sound minor, but they fundamentally change how the peptide behaves in your body.\n\nTo understand why those modifications matter, you need to understand what normally happens to IGF-1. Your liver produces natural IGF-1 in response to growth hormone signaling. Once released, the vast majority of that IGF-1 gets immediately grabbed by a group of proteins called insulin-like growth factor binding proteins, or IGFBPs. These binding proteins act like chaperones. They carry IGF-1 through your bloodstream, regulate how much is available to interact with receptors, and ultimately limit its half-life to roughly 12 to 15 hours.\n\nIGF-1 LR3's structural modifications give it extremely low affinity for those binding proteins. That means it circulates in a much more active, unbound state. The result is roughly 3 times greater potency than native IGF-1 and a significantly extended half-life of 20 to 30 hours. You are getting a version of IGF-1 that stays active longer and hits harder because nothing is holding it back.\n\nIGF-1 LR3 is not approved by the FDA for any medical use. Clinically, the only FDA-approved indication for direct IGF-1 is a rare genetic condition where growth hormone receptors are broken and the liver cannot convert GH into IGF-1. …","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent community consensus and clinical practice patterns.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\nDose:           40 to 80 mcg daily\nFrequency:      Once daily, post-workout preferred\nTiming:         Within 15 to 30 minutes after training\nAdministration: Subcutaneous or intramuscular\nCycle length:   4 to 6 weeks on, 4 to 6 weeks off\n\nBEGINNER PROTOCOL\nWeek 1:         20 to 40 mcg daily (assess tolerance)\nWeek 2 to 4:    40 to 60 mcg daily\nWeek 5 to 6:    Maintain or taper down\nAfter cycle:    Minimum 4 weeks off before repeating\n\nRECONSTITUTION\nVial size:      1 mg\nSolvent:        0.6% acetic acid solution (NOT bacteriostatic water)\nVolume:         1 mL of acetic acid\nConcentration:  100 mcg per 10 units on insulin syringe\nStorage:        Refrigerate at 2 to 8 C, use within 28 days\n\nDo not reconstitute with bacteriostatic water. The benzyl alcohol in bac water degrades the IGF-1 LR3 peptide structure within 24 to 48 hours. Acetic acid maintains a mildly acidic pH that supports the peptide's structural stability through the full vial.\n\nTiming Considerations\nPost-workout is the most common timing to capitalize on the training stimulus and the enhanced nutrient uptake into muscle cells. Always have fast-acting carbohydrates and protein available after injection. The insulin-like effects of IGF-1 LR3 can lower blood sugar quickly, and combining it with post-workout nutrition both mitigates hypoglycemia risk and supports the anabolic environment.\n\nCritical Notes\nHypoglycemia is the most significant acute risk. Always have fast-acting carbohydrates available. Many users consume carbohydrates and protein shortly after injection. …","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":40,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.04}],"strengths":[0.1,1]},{"name":"Ipamorelin","protocolName":"Ipamorelin","category":"Growth Hormone Family","overview":"Ipamorelin is a synthetic growth hormone releasing peptide that signals your pituitary gland to produce and release more of your own growth hormone. It belongs to a class of compounds called growth hormone secretagogues, which means it stimulates natural production rather than introducing an external hormone directly into your body.\n\nWhat makes ipamorelin stand out from every other peptide in its class is selectivity. Your body has multiple hormonal systems that can be affected when you stimulate the ghrelin receptor, and older peptides like GHRP-2 and GHRP-6 activate all of them. That means when you take those peptides, you get growth hormone release, but you also get spikes in cortisol (your stress hormone), prolactin (which can cause low libido and breast tissue changes in men), and intense hunger. Ipamorelin avoids all of that. In the landmark study that established its profile, even at doses more than 200 times higher than what was needed for growth hormone release, ipamorelin did not significantly increase ACTH, cortisol, or prolactin. That level of selectivity is why researchers called it the first selective growth hormone secretagogue.\n\nIpamorelin is a pentapeptide, meaning it is made up of just five amino acids. It was originally developed by Novo Nordisk in Denmark. It reached Phase 2 clinical trials for post-operative ileus (gut motility after surgery) but did not meet efficacy endpoints, and development was discontinued. It is not FDA approved for any human use. It remains available through research peptide suppliers.","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL (COMBINED WITH CJC-1295 NO DAC)\nIpamorelin:      100 to 200 mcg\nCJC-1295 no DAC: 100 to 200 mcg\nFrequency:       Once daily before bed\nAdministration:  Subcutaneous injection\nFasting:         At least 2 hours after eating, wait 30 minutes after injection before eating\nSchedule:        Daily\nDuration:        12 to 16 weeks, then 4 weeks off\n\nSOLO PROTOCOL\nDose:       200 to 300 mcg per injection\nFrequency:  1 to 2 times daily\nTiming:     Before bed and/or morning (fasted)\nSchedule:   Daily\nDuration:   12 to 16 weeks, then 4 weeks off\n\nSPLIT DOSING PROTOCOL (MAXIMUM PULSATILITY)\nMorning (fasted): 100 to 150 mcg Ipamorelin + 100 to 150 mcg CJC-1295\nBefore bed:       100 to 150 mcg Ipamorelin + 100 to 150 mcg CJC-1295\nThis creates two separate growth hormone pulses per day.\n\nBY BODY WEIGHT\nUnder 150 lbs:  100 to 150 mcg per injection\n150 to 200 lbs: 150 to 200 mcg per injection\nOver 200 lbs:   200 to 300 mcg per injection\n\nRECONSTITUTION (standard)\nVial size:     5 mg\nBac water:     2 mL\nConcentration: 250 mcg per 10 units on insulin syringe\n\nFor a CJC/Ipamorelin blend vial (5 mg CJC + 5 mg Ipamorelin in one vial), add 2 mL bac water. Draw 4 units on an insulin syringe for 100 mcg of each compound per dose.\n\nWhy the Combination Matters\nThe combination of a GHRH analog (CJC-1295) plus a ghrelin agonist (ipamorelin) produces significantly more growth hormone release than either peptide alone. This synergistic effect occurs because they work through two different pathways. The GHRH analog increases the production capacity (telling the factory to make more). …","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":200,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.2}],"strengths":[2,5,10]},{"name":"Kisspeptin-10","protocolName":"Kisspeptin-10","category":"Hormone / Fertility","overview":"Kisspeptin-10 is a synthetic fragment of the naturally occurring kisspeptin peptide. The full-length kisspeptin (kisspeptin-54) is produced by neurons in your hypothalamus, and it plays a critical role in regulating your reproductive hormone axis. Kisspeptin-10 is the biologically active 10 amino acid fragment derived from the C-terminal end of kisspeptin-54, and it retains the full receptor-binding activity of the larger peptide.\n\nTo understand why kisspeptin matters, you need to understand the HPG axis. Your hypothalamus releases GnRH (gonadotropin-releasing hormone), which travels to your pituitary gland and tells it to release LH (luteinizing hormone) and FSH (follicle-stimulating hormone). LH then travels to your testes and tells them to produce testosterone. FSH tells them to produce sperm. This is the three-stage signaling cascade that controls testosterone production in men.\n\nKisspeptin sits above all of this. It is the signal that tells your GnRH neurons to fire. Without kisspeptin signaling, your GnRH neurons do not activate, your pituitary does not release LH and FSH, and your testes do not produce testosterone or sperm. Kisspeptin is essentially the master switch at the very top of the reproductive axis.\n\nThis is why I talk about kisspeptin in the same way I talk about GHRH analogs like CJC-1295 or tesamorelin for growth hormone. Those peptides work at the top of the GH axis by stimulating your body's natural GH release. Kisspeptin works at the top of the testosterone axis by stimulating your body's natural GnRH release. The principle is the same. The higher up the axis you intervene, the more you preserve your body's natural feedback mechanisms. …","schedule":"Practical Protocols\n\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\n\nDose:           100 to 200 mcg subcutaneously\n\nFrequency:      Every other day\n\nTiming:         Morning or evening (no fasting required)\n\nDuration:       4 to 8 weeks per cycle\n\nLab check:      Baseline and 4 weeks after starting\n\nBY BODY WEIGHT\n\nUnder 150 lbs:  100 mcg every other day\n\n150 to 200 lbs: 100 to 150 mcg every other day\n\nOver 200 lbs:   150 to 200 mcg every other day\n\nWHAT TO MONITOR ON LABS\n\nLH:             Confirm kisspeptin is stimulating GnRH release\n\nTotal and free testosterone:  Confirm downstream response\n\nEstradiol:      Monitor aromatization as testosterone increases\n\nFSH:            Confirm gonadotropin response\n\nRECONSTITUTION (standard)\n\nVial size:      5 mg\n\nBac water:      2 mL\n\nConcentration:  250 mcg per 10 units on insulin syringe\n\n100 mcg = 4 units\n\n150 mcg = 6 units\n\n200 mcg = 8 units","frequency":"Every other day","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":100,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.1}],"strengths":[5,10]},{"name":"KPV","protocolName":"KPV","category":"Recovery / Repair","overview":"KPV is a tripeptide composed of three amino acids: Lysine, Proline, and Valine. It is derived from the C-terminal end of alpha-melanocyte stimulating hormone. Researchers discovered that when they broke alpha-MSH down into fragments, the anti-inflammatory activity could be traced specifically to this three amino acid sequence at the tail end of the molecule.\n\nWhat makes KPV interesting is what it does not do. Alpha-MSH activates melanocortin receptors, which is how compounds like MT-1 and MT-2 darken your skin. KPV does not bind to melanocortin receptors at all. It delivers the anti-inflammatory properties of the parent hormone without any effect on skin pigmentation, appetite, or sexual function. It is a \"clean\" anti-inflammatory peptide that works through an entirely different pathway than what most people expect from an alpha-MSH derivative.\n\nKPV is one of the smallest peptides in active research, at only three amino acids and approximately 325 daltons in molecular weight. Its small size is actually an advantage because it can be absorbed through multiple routes: oral, subcutaneous injection, and topical application. This versatility makes it particularly practical for different use cases, especially gut inflammation where oral delivery puts the compound directly where it is needed.\n\nAs of early 2026, all published research on KPV is preclinical. There are no published human clinical trials. The evidence comes from cell culture studies and animal models, primarily mouse models of colitis. The preclinical evidence is promising, but anyone using KPV should understand that we are working with animal data, not human data.","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published human dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nSUBCUTANEOUS INJECTION (Systemic Anti-Inflammatory)\nDose:           200 to 400 mcg once daily\nDuration:       4 to 8 weeks\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection\nUse case:       Systemic inflammation, autoimmune support\n\nORAL ADMINISTRATION (Gut Focus)\nDose:           200 to 500 mcg once or twice daily\nDuration:       4 to 8 weeks, can extend with monitoring\nAdministration: Oral capsule or solution\nUse case:       Gut inflammation, intestinal permeability, IBD support\nNote:           Oral delivery is supported by the PepT1 mechanism\n\nTOPICAL APPLICATION (Skin)\nConcentration:  0.01 to 0.1% in cream base\nFrequency:      Twice daily\nDuration:       As needed\nUse case:       Eczema, psoriasis, wound healing\nNote:           Better penetration on compromised or inflamed skin\n\nReconstitution (for injectable form)\nRECONSTITUTION (standard)\nVial size:      5 mg\nBac water:      1 mL\nConcentration:  250 mcg per 5 units on insulin syringe\nStorage:        Refrigerate after reconstitution","frequency":"Twice daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":200,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.2}],"strengths":[5,10,30,80]},{"name":"LL-37","protocolName":"LL-37","category":"Recovery / Repair","overview":"LL-37 is the only cathelicidin antimicrobial peptide produced by the human body. It is a 37 amino acid peptide that serves as one of your immune system's primary weapons against bacteria, viruses, fungi, and parasites. The name comes from its structure: it starts with two leucine amino acids (LL) and is 37 amino acids long.\n\nYour body produces LL-37 from a precursor protein called hCAP-18 (human cationic antimicrobial protein 18 kDa). When your immune system detects a threat, enzymes cleave hCAP-18 to release the active LL-37 peptide. This happens in neutrophils, macrophages, dendritic cells, natural killer cells, and epithelial cells throughout your body, particularly in the skin, lungs, and gastrointestinal tract.\n\nWhat makes LL-37 remarkable is its versatility. Unlike traditional antibiotics that target one specific mechanism, LL-37 kills pathogens through multiple pathways, modulates your immune response, neutralizes bacterial toxins, promotes wound healing, and stimulates new blood vessel formation. This multi-functionality makes it difficult for pathogens to develop resistance against it, which is a fundamental advantage over conventional antibiotics.\n\nLL-37 has gained significant attention as antibiotic resistance becomes an increasingly serious global health threat. The World Health Organization has identified antimicrobial resistance as one of the top ten threats to global health. LL-37 represents a fundamentally different approach to fighting infection because it works with your immune system rather than simply poisoning bacteria the way traditional antibiotics do.\n\nClinical trials have tested LL-37 topically for wound healing in both venous leg ulcers and diabetic foot ulcers with positive results. …","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published human dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus for injectable use.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\nDose:           100 to 200 mcg per day\nFrequency:      Once daily\nTiming:         No specific timing requirements\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection\nDuration:       2 to 4 weeks for acute immune support, 4 to 6 weeks for chronic issues\n\nBY APPLICATION\nAcute infection support:       200 mcg daily for 5 to 10 days\nChronic / biofilm infection:   100 to 200 mcg daily for 4 to 6 weeks\nImmune system support:         100 mcg daily for 2 to 4 weeks\n\nSome practitioners use 125 mcg daily for up to 50 days, followed by a 2 to 4 week break before repeating if needed.\n\nReconstitution\nRECONSTITUTION (standard)\nVial size:      5 mg\nBac water:      2 mL\nConcentration:  250 mcg per 10 units on insulin syringe\n\nDRAW VOLUMES (at 2 mL reconstitution)\n100 mcg:   4 units (0.04 mL)\n125 mcg:   5 units (0.05 mL)\n200 mcg:   8 units (0.08 mL)\n250 mcg:   10 units (0.10 mL)\n\nAt 200 mcg daily, a 5 mg vial provides 25 days of dosing.\n\nWhy Dose Conservative\nLL-37 is a potent immune stimulator. Excessive dosing can cause significant local injection site reactions, flu-like symptoms from cytokine release, potential aggravation of autoimmune conditions, and host cell cytotoxicity at high concentrations. The clinical trial data showing the lower dose (0.5 mg/mL) outperforming the higher dose (1.6 mg/mL) in wound healing reinforces the principle that more is not better with this peptide. …","frequency":"5 days per week","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":100,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.1}],"strengths":[5,10]},{"name":"MGF","protocolName":"MGF","category":"IGF / Growth","overview":"MGF stands for Mechano Growth Factor. It is a splice variant of Insulin-like Growth Factor 1 (IGF-1) that your body produces locally in muscle tissue when that tissue experiences mechanical stress or damage from resistance training. Its scientific name is IGF-1Ec in humans and IGF-1Eb in rodents.\n\nWhen you lift weights and create micro-tears in muscle fibers, your body splices the IGF-1 gene differently than it normally would. Instead of producing the standard circulating form of IGF-1, it produces MGF right at the site of damage. This is one of the first signals your body sends to begin the repair process.\n\nWhat makes MGF structurally unique is a 49 base pair insert in the gene that creates a 24 amino acid C-terminal peptide sequence not found in regular IGF-1. This unique E-domain peptide is what gives MGF its distinct biological activity separate from standard IGF-1 signaling.\n\nThe synthetic version replicates this naturally occurring peptide. It is sold as a research compound and is not approved by the FDA for human use. MGF is banned by the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA) under the category of peptide hormones and growth factors, and it is also banned by the UFC, NFL, NCAA, NHL, MLB, NAIA, and PGA.\n\nThe most important thing to understand before going further is that MGF has an extremely short half-life of approximately 5 to 7 minutes. This creates significant practical limitations. Your body naturally produces MGF right at the site of muscle damage, so it does not need to survive long in circulation. But when you inject synthetic MGF, it degrades almost immediately. This is why many practitioners prefer PEG-MGF, which is the pegylated version covered in a separate article.","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\nDose:           200 to 400 mcg per injection\nFrequency:      2 to 3 times per week on training days only\nTiming:         Immediately post-workout, within 15 minutes of finishing\nAdministration: Intramuscular injection into the trained muscle group\nCycle length:   4 to 6 weeks\nMax weekly dose: Do not exceed 2 mg per week\nPRACTICAL PROTOCOL EXAMPLE\nMonday:    Train chest. Immediately after, inject 200 to 400 mcg IM into chest.\nWednesday: Train back. Immediately after, inject 200 to 400 mcg IM into lats.\nFriday:    Train legs. Immediately after, inject 200 to 400 mcg IM into quads.\nRECONSTITUTION (standard)\nVial size:      2 mg\nBac water:      1 mL\nConcentration:  200 mcg per 10 units on insulin syringe\nWhy Intramuscular Injection Matters\nUnlike most peptides where subcutaneous injection into the abdomen works fine, MGF requires intramuscular injection directly into the muscle you just trained. The 5 to 7 minute half-life means the peptide must reach the target tissue before it degrades. Injecting subcutaneously into your abdomen after a leg workout means the MGF will break down before it ever reaches your quads. This is not a preference. It is a necessity dictated by the pharmacokinetics of this peptide.","frequency":"Weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":200,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.2}],"strengths":[2]},{"name":"MOTS-C","protocolName":"MOTS-C","category":"Mitochondrial / Longevity","overview":"MOTS-c stands for Mitochondrial Open Reading Frame of the 12S rRNA type-c. It is a 16 amino acid peptide that your mitochondria actually produce naturally. Unlike most peptides that come from instructions in your cell's nucleus, MOTS-c is encoded directly in your mitochondrial DNA. That is rare, and it makes this peptide very unique.\n\nYour body already makes MOTS-c during exercise and fasting. It acts as a mitochondrial hormone that circulates in your bloodstream and tells your cells how to use energy. When MOTS-c is released, it activates AMPK, which is the metabolic master switch inside your cells. That switch tells your body to start burning fat, taking up glucose more efficiently, and building new healthier mitochondria.\n\nMOTS-c was first identified in 2015 by researchers at the University of Southern California who were studying the mitochondrial genome. Since then, about 200 preclinical studies have been published examining its effects on metabolism, aging, exercise physiology, bone health, cardiovascular function, and more. Despite all that research, direct human clinical data on MOTS-c itself is extremely limited. The closest human evidence comes from CB4211, a synthetic stabilized analog developed by CohBar, which completed a Phase 1a/1b trial in 2021 with positive safety and early efficacy signals.\n\nIt is not approved by the FDA for human use. In 2024, WADA added MOTS-c to the Prohibited List under section S4.4 (Metabolic Modulators) as an AMPK activator. It remains prohibited in 2025 and 2026 and is banned in all WADA-governed sports.","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\n``` STANDARD PROTOCOL Dose: 5 to 10 mg per week total Frequency: Split into 3 injections per week (Monday, Wednesday, Friday) Timing: Morning on an empty stomach, or 30 minutes before training Administration: Subcutaneous injection Duration: 4 to 8 week cycles followed by equal time off ```\n\n``` BY GOAL Training enhancement (active exerciser): 5 to 10 mg per week, 3 injections Metabolic support (insulin resistance): 10 to 15 mg per week, 3 injections Longevity and general wellness: 5 to 10 mg per week, 3 injections, 4 to 8 week cycles ```\n\n``` RECONSTITUTION (standard) Vial size: 5 mg or 10 mg Bac water: 1 mL per 5 mg vial (5 mg/mL concentration) Storage: Lyophilized at -20C, reconstituted at 2 to 8C Stability: Reconstituted MOTS-c remains stable for approximately 14 to 30 days refrigerated ```\n\nDosing Frequency Matters\nBased on the exercise data, your circulating MOTS-c levels return to baseline within hours. …","frequency":"Weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":5,"unit":"mg","mg":5}],"strengths":[5,10,15,20,30,40]},{"name":"NAD+","protocolName":"NAD+","category":"Mitochondrial / Longevity","overview":"NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) is a molecule found in every cell of your body that plays a central role in energy production, DNA repair, and cellular health. It is not a peptide. It is a coenzyme, which means it helps enzymes do their jobs. Without adequate NAD+, your cells cannot produce energy efficiently, repair damaged DNA, or maintain the hundreds of metabolic reactions that keep you functioning.\n\nMost people hear about NAD+ as an anti-aging supplement, but before you decide whether it is for you, you need to understand what it actually does at the cellular level, what happens when it declines, and the different ways to address that decline.\n\nEvery single thing your body does requires energy, and that energy comes from ATP. Your cells use ATP to contract muscles, fire neurons, repair tissue, and keep you alive. Your body stores almost zero ATP. You only have about 100 grams on hand at any given time, which is basically enough energy for just a few seconds of intense activity. So your body has to constantly remake ATP all day, every day. A moderately active person cycles through their entire body weight in ATP daily.\n\nNAD+ is essential for that ATP production process. It is a critical cofactor in the electron transport chain, which is where about 90% of your ATP gets made. When NAD+ levels drop, your energy production slows down, and that affects everything downstream.\n\nThe problem is that NAD+ levels decline significantly with age. By the time you reach your 40s, your NAD+ levels may have dropped by 50% or more compared to when you were young. This decline is associated with reduced energy, impaired cellular repair, metabolic dysfunction, and many of the hallmarks of aging. …","schedule":"Understanding the Different Approaches\nUnlike some peptides that only work when dysfunction is present, NAD+ decline affects everyone with age. By your 40s, NAD+ levels may have dropped 50% or more from youthful levels. This means most adults over 40 can potentially benefit from NAD+ support regardless of whether they have symptoms.\n\nSubcutaneous Injection\nLOADING PROTOCOL\nDose:           100 to 200 mg daily\nDuration:       7 to 10 consecutive days\nPurpose:        Rapidly restore depleted cellular reserves\n\nMAINTENANCE PROTOCOL\nDose:           50 to 100 mg\nFrequency:      1 to 3 times per week\nTiming:         Morning preferred (may interfere with sleep if taken late)\n\nIntramuscular Injection\n50 to 100 mg, 1 to 3 times per week. Similar dosing to subcutaneous but absorbed slightly faster.\n\nOral Precursors\nNIACIN (Vitamin B3)\nStarting dose:  100 to 250 mg daily (build tolerance for flush)\nStandard dose:  500 to 1000 mg daily\nNote:           Cheapest and most proven oral option\nLimitation:     Niacin flush (redness, warmth, itching for 20-30 min)\n\nNMN (Nicotinamide Mononucleotide)\nStarting dose:  250 to 500 mg daily\nStandard dose:  500 to 1000 mg daily\nNote:           More expensive, may largely convert to niacin in gut\nLimitation:     NMN regulatory status uncertain (FDA flagged in 2022)\n\nNR (Nicotinamide Riboside)\nStarting dose:  250 to 500 mg daily\nStandard dose:  500 to 1000 mg daily\nNote:           Available as dietary supplement (Tru Niagen, etc.)\nLimitation:     Similar gut conversion concerns as NMN\n\nCycling (Non-Negotiable for Ongoing Use)\nDo 8 to 12 weeks on NAD+ supplementation, then 4 to 8 weeks off. This prevents NAMPT suppression and gives your body's natural production pathways a chance to recover. Exercise during the off-cycle helps restore natural NAD+ production.\n\nReconstitution (Injectable)\nNAD+ requires more bacteriostatic water than most peptides. It is a large molecule that can crystallize or precipitate out of solution when the concentration is too high. The standard 2 mL reconstitution used for most peptides does not work here.\n\nRECONSTITUTION\n500 mg vial:    Add 3 mL bacteriostatic water\nConcentration: 166.7 mg/mL …","frequency":"Weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":100,"unit":"mg","mg":100},{"value":50,"unit":"mg","mg":50},{"value":500,"unit":"mg","mg":500},{"value":250,"unit":"mg","mg":250}],"strengths":[100,500,1000]},{"name":"Oxytocin Acetate","protocolName":"Oxytocin","category":"Sexual Wellness / Tanning","overview":"Oxytocin is a 9 amino acid peptide hormone that your body produces naturally. It is synthesized in the hypothalamus, specifically in the paraventricular nucleus and supraoptic nucleus, and released by the posterior pituitary gland into the bloodstream. It is one of the oldest and most conserved signaling molecules in mammalian biology.\n\nOxytocin is often called the \"love hormone\" or \"bonding hormone,\" and while those names oversimplify what it does, they point in the right direction. Your body releases oxytocin during physical touch, sexual activity, orgasm, childbirth, breastfeeding, and positive social interactions. It plays a role in pair bonding, trust, empathy, stress regulation, and social cognition.\n\nBut oxytocin is not just a feel-good molecule. It has real physiological effects beyond mood. It plays a role in sexual function, cortisol regulation, inflammation, pain modulation, and even wound healing. In the context of sexual health and performance, oxytocin is relevant because of its role in arousal, orgasm, ejaculation, and the emotional connection that makes sex satisfying.\n\nOxytocin is FDA approved in its injectable form (Pitocin) for inducing labor and controlling postpartum hemorrhage. Intranasal oxytocin for mood, bonding, sexual function, or metabolic purposes is not FDA approved and remains investigational. Compounding pharmacies offer oxytocin in various forms including intranasal sprays, sublingual troches, and subcutaneous injections.","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies for sexual health. They represent clinical practice patterns and published research doses adapted by practitioners.\n\nINTRANASAL PROTOCOL (most common for central effects)\nDose:           20 to 40 IU per administration\nTiming:         20 to 30 minutes before desired effect\nFrequency:      As needed or daily for stress management\nAdministration: Intranasal spray, alternating nostrils\n\nSUBLINGUAL TROCHE PROTOCOL\nDose:           Prescribed by physician, typically 10 to 100 IU\nTiming:         30 to 45 minutes before desired effect\nFrequency:      As needed\nAdministration: Dissolve under tongue\n\nSUBCUTANEOUS PROTOCOL (peripheral effects)\nDose:           4 to 10 mcg\nTiming:         30 minutes before desired effect\nFrequency:      As needed\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection\nNote:           Primarily peripheral effects; limited CNS penetration","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":4,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.004}],"strengths":[2,5,10]},{"name":"PEG-MGF","protocolName":"PEG-MGF","category":"IGF / Growth","overview":"PEG-MGF is the pegylated version of Mechano Growth Factor (MGF). It is the same 24 amino acid C-terminal E-domain peptide derived from the IGF-1Ec splice variant, but with polyethylene glycol (PEG) chains chemically attached to the peptide structure.\n\nMGF is a splice variant of IGF-1 that your body produces locally in muscle tissue after mechanical stress or damage from resistance training. Its job is to activate satellite cells, the muscle stem cells that drive repair and growth. The problem with natural MGF and its synthetic counterpart is an extremely short half-life of approximately 5 to 7 minutes. The peptide degrades almost instantly in circulation, which makes the standard version impractical for most users.\n\nPEGylation solves this problem. By attaching PEG molecules to the peptide, the modified version resists enzymatic breakdown in the bloodstream. The PEG chains create a hydrophilic shield around the peptide that physically blocks the proteolytic enzymes that would normally break it down within minutes. This extends the functional half-life from approximately 5 to 7 minutes to an estimated several hours, with some sources citing up to 48 to 72 hours based on the pharmacokinetic principles of PEGylation. The PEG group itself does not bind to other substances in the body and is excreted through urination.\n\nThis is a research compound. It is not approved by the FDA for human use. PEG-MGF is banned by the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA) under the category of peptide hormones and growth factors, and it is also banned by the UFC, NFL, NCAA, NHL, MLB, NAIA, and PGA.\n\nHere is the most important thing to understand about PEG-MGF upfront. There are zero published studies specifically on PEG-MGF in any context, human or animal. …","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\nDose:           200 to 400 mcg per injection\nFrequency:      2 to 3 times per week on training days\nTiming:         Post-workout (no rush, within a few hours is fine)\nAdministration: Intramuscular injection into the trained muscle (preferred)\nor subcutaneous injection\nCycle length:   4 to 6 weeks\n\nBY APPLICATION\nTargeted muscle growth:     200 to 400 mcg IM into the trained muscle, 2 to 3x per week\nGeneral recovery support:   200 mcg subQ post-workout, 2 to 3x per week\nAdvanced protocol:          400 mcg IM on training days, alternating muscle groups\n\nPRACTICAL PROTOCOL EXAMPLE\nMonday:    Train chest. Inject 200 to 400 mcg IM into chest or subQ.\nWednesday: Train back. Inject 200 to 400 mcg IM into lats or subQ.\nFriday:    Train legs. Inject 200 to 400 mcg IM into quads or subQ.\n\nRECONSTITUTION (standard)\nVial size:      2 mg\nBac water:      1 mL\nConcentration:  200 mcg per 10 units on insulin syringe\n\nIntramuscular vs Subcutaneous\nUnlike standard MGF where intramuscular injection is essentially mandatory, PEG-MGF gives you the option of either route. Intramuscular injection into the trained muscle is still preferred by most practitioners who are targeting specific muscle groups, since it delivers the highest local concentration at the target tissue. Subcutaneous injection is a valid option for general recovery support and is more convenient and less painful. The extended half-life means the peptide has time to circulate and reach muscle tissue even from a subcutaneous injection site.","frequency":"Weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":200,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.2}],"strengths":[2]},{"name":"Pinealon","protocolName":"Pinealon","category":"Cognitive / Nootropic / Sleep","overview":"Pinealon is a synthetic tripeptide made up of three amino acids: L-glutamic acid, L-aspartic acid, and L-arginine. The amino acid sequence is Glu-Asp-Arg, which is why you will also see it called \"EDR peptide\" in the research literature. It was originally isolated from Cortexin, a neuroprotective drug derived from pig brain cortex tissue, and then synthesized in its short-chain form in Russia.\n\nPinealon belongs to a class of compounds called peptide bioregulators. These are short-chain peptides developed primarily at the St. Petersburg Institute of Bioregulation and Gerontology, led by Vladimir Khavinson, who has been the principal researcher behind most of the published work on this compound. As the name suggests, Pinealon targets the pineal gland and the central nervous system.\n\nWhat makes Pinealon unusual among peptides is its size. At just three amino acids, it is one of the smallest bioactive peptides studied for brain health. This tiny structure is what allows it to cross the blood-brain barrier efficiently and, according to the research, penetrate into cell nuclei where it may interact directly with DNA. That nuclear access is the core of how it works, and I will walk you through that mechanism in detail.\n\nPinealon is not FDA approved. It remains an experimental compound with most research coming from preclinical studies and limited clinical observations conducted primarily in Russia. No large-scale Western clinical trials have been conducted.","schedule":"Practical Protocols\n\nThe following protocols are based on practitioner experience and bioregulator framework conventions. They are not derived from published dose-finding studies. No published data establishes the optimal dose, frequency, or cycle length for Pinealon in humans.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\n\nDose:           100 to 300 mcg per day\n\nFrequency:      Once daily\n\nTiming:         Morning (to align with circadian rhythms)\n\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection or intranasal\n\nDuration:       10 to 20 days per cycle\n\nRest period:    2 to 3 months between cycles\n\nBY APPLICATION\n\nGeneral neuroprotection:    100 to 200 mcg daily for 10 to 20 days\n\nCognitive support:          200 to 300 mcg daily for 10 to 20 days\n\nSleep and circadian reset:  100 to 200 mcg daily for 10 to 20 days\n\nRECONSTITUTION (standard)\n\nVial size:      10 mg\n\nBac water:      2 mL\n\nConcentration:  500 mcg per 10 units on insulin syringe\n\nAt 200 mcg/day: 50 doses per vial (well beyond a single cycle)\n\nCycling Rationale\n\nThe 10 to 20 day on, 2 to 3 month off cycling protocol is the standard convention for Khavinson bioregulator peptides. …","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":100,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.1}],"strengths":[5,10,20]},{"name":"PT-141","protocolName":"PT-141","category":"Sexual Wellness / Tanning","overview":"PT-141, also known as bremelanotide, is a synthetic peptide consisting of 7 amino acids. It is a melanocortin receptor agonist derived from Melanotan II, but engineered to be more selective. Where Melanotan II activates multiple melanocortin receptors and produces tanning, appetite suppression, and sexual effects all at once, PT-141 was developed to isolate the sexual function component.\n\nYour body has five melanocortin receptors, MC1 through MC5. PT-141 primarily activates the MC4 receptor, which is the receptor in your brain responsible for regulating sexual arousal and desire. It also has some activity at MC3 and MC1, but the sexual effects are driven by MC4 activation in the hypothalamus.\n\nHere is the important distinction between PT-141 and drugs like Viagra or Cialis. Those drugs are PDE5 inhibitors. They work on blood flow. They affect the physical mechanics of an erection by relaxing smooth muscle in the blood vessels of the penis. PT-141 does not work on blood flow at all. It works through your central nervous system by activating receptors in your brain that govern sexual desire and arousal. That means it addresses the want, not just the physical ability. This is why PT-141 works in both men and women, and why it can help people who do not respond to Viagra or Cialis.\n\nPT-141 was originally developed by Palatin Technologies. Its discovery came from Melanotan II research at the University of Arizona in the 1990s, when a researcher accidentally injected twice his intended dose of Melanotan II and experienced an 8-hour erection. That observation led to the isolation and development of PT-141 as a more targeted compound for sexual function. …","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\nDose:           1 to 2 mg subcutaneously\nFrequency:      As needed, 45 to 60 minutes before sexual activity\nMaximum:        One dose per 24 hours, 8 doses per month\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection (abdomen or thigh)\n\nFOR NAUSEA-SENSITIVE INDIVIDUALS\nStart:          0.5 mg for the first use\nIncrease:       0.75 mg, then 1 mg on subsequent uses\nGoal:           Find minimum effective dose with tolerable nausea\nTip:            Ondansetron (Zofran) 30 minutes prior can reduce nausea\n\nRECONSTITUTION (standard)\nVial size:      10 mg\nBac water:      2 mL\nConcentration:  500 mcg per 10 units on insulin syringe\n1 mg = 20 units\n1.75 mg = 35 units\n2 mg = 40 units","frequency":"See recovered schedule","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":1,"unit":"mg","mg":1}],"strengths":[10]},{"name":"Retatrutide","protocolName":"Retatrutide","category":"Weight Loss / Metabolic","overview":"Retatrutide is a triple hormone receptor agonist developed by Eli Lilly that simultaneously activates three receptor systems: GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1), GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide), and glucagon. This triple action is what separates it from every other weight loss compound currently available or in development.\n\nTo put the differences in context, semaglutide activates one receptor (GLP-1) and produces roughly 15% average weight loss. Tirzepatide activates two receptors (GLP-1 and GIP) and produces roughly 20% average weight loss. Retatrutide activates all three and in Phase 2 trials produced 24.2% average weight loss at 48 weeks. Phase 3 data confirmed this, showing 28.7% average weight loss at 68 weeks at the highest dose.\n\nThe compound is administered as a once-weekly subcutaneous injection. It has a half-life of approximately 6 days, which supports that weekly dosing schedule.\n\nAs of early 2026, retatrutide is not FDA approved. It is currently in Phase 3 clinical trials through the TRIUMPH program, which enrolls over 5,800 participants across four studies. The first Phase 3 results from TRIUMPH-4 were announced in December 2025. FDA submission is planned for late 2026 or early 2027, with potential approval in late 2027.\n\nEli Lilly has pursued a biologics license application rather than a standard drug application, which would grant up to 12 years of market exclusivity versus 5 years. They are also building a $27 billion manufacturing facility in anticipation of demand.","schedule":"TITRATION SCHEDULE\nWeeks 1 to 4:      2 mg once weekly\nWeeks 5 to 8:      4 mg once weekly\nWeeks 9 to 12:     8 mg once weekly\nWeek 13 onward:    12 mg once weekly (maximum dose)\n\nIMPORTANT NOTES\nStarting at 2 mg instead of 4 mg makes a real difference in tolerability.\nMany people get excellent results at 8 mg without needing 12 mg.\nIf side effects are rough at any step, stay at that dose for another 4 weeks.\nThere is no benefit to pushing through severe nausea.\nTake your injection on the same day each week.\n\nNot Everyone Needs Maximum Dose\nConsider your actual goal:\n\nIf 15 to 20% weight loss achieves your target: 4 to 8 mg may be sufficient\n\nIf you need 20% or more: 8 to 12 mg may benefit you\n\nIf you previously used semaglutide or tirzepatide: retatrutide at moderate doses may produce results you could not achieve at maximum doses of those medications\n\nSplit Dosing\nSome people split their weekly dose into two injections (for example, 1 mg twice per week instead of 2 mg once per week). This compresses the peak-to-trough ratio and keeps serum levels more stable. The total weekly dose drives steady state concentration, not injection frequency. So 2 mg once a week and 1 mg twice a week produce the same average steady-state concentration. Split dosing can help manage GI side effects and the peak-related skin sensitivity.\n\nThis is different from microdosing. In pharmacology, microdosing means less than 1/100th of the dose that produces a pharmacological effect. What most people describe as microdosing retatrutide is actually split dosing. Actual microdosing of retatrutide does not make sense because you would never reach a steady-state concentration high enough to produce meaningful results.\n\nReconstitution\nThe reconstitution depends on your vial size and desired concentration. Work with your specific vial to calculate the concentration based on total peptide content divided by the volume of bacteriostatic water added.","frequency":"Twice weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":4,"unit":"mg","mg":4},{"value":8,"unit":"mg","mg":8}],"strengths":[5,10,15,20,30,40,50,60,100]},{"name":"Retatrutide + Cagrilintide","protocolName":"Retatrutide","category":"Weight Loss / Metabolic","overview":"Retatrutide is a triple hormone receptor agonist developed by Eli Lilly that simultaneously activates three receptor systems: GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1), GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide), and glucagon. This triple action is what separates it from every other weight loss compound currently available or in development.\n\nTo put the differences in context, semaglutide activates one receptor (GLP-1) and produces roughly 15% average weight loss. Tirzepatide activates two receptors (GLP-1 and GIP) and produces roughly 20% average weight loss. Retatrutide activates all three and in Phase 2 trials produced 24.2% average weight loss at 48 weeks. Phase 3 data confirmed this, showing 28.7% average weight loss at 68 weeks at the highest dose.\n\nThe compound is administered as a once-weekly subcutaneous injection. It has a half-life of approximately 6 days, which supports that weekly dosing schedule.\n\nAs of early 2026, retatrutide is not FDA approved. It is currently in Phase 3 clinical trials through the TRIUMPH program, which enrolls over 5,800 participants across four studies. The first Phase 3 results from TRIUMPH-4 were announced in December 2025. FDA submission is planned for late 2026 or early 2027, with potential approval in late 2027.\n\nEli Lilly has pursued a biologics license application rather than a standard drug application, which would grant up to 12 years of market exclusivity versus 5 years. They are also building a $27 billion manufacturing facility in anticipation of demand.","schedule":"TITRATION SCHEDULE\nWeeks 1 to 4:      2 mg once weekly\nWeeks 5 to 8:      4 mg once weekly\nWeeks 9 to 12:     8 mg once weekly\nWeek 13 onward:    12 mg once weekly (maximum dose)\n\nIMPORTANT NOTES\nStarting at 2 mg instead of 4 mg makes a real difference in tolerability.\nMany people get excellent results at 8 mg without needing 12 mg.\nIf side effects are rough at any step, stay at that dose for another 4 weeks.\nThere is no benefit to pushing through severe nausea.\nTake your injection on the same day each week.\n\nNot Everyone Needs Maximum Dose\nConsider your actual goal:\n\nIf 15 to 20% weight loss achieves your target: 4 to 8 mg may be sufficient\n\nIf you need 20% or more: 8 to 12 mg may benefit you\n\nIf you previously used semaglutide or tirzepatide: retatrutide at moderate doses may produce results you could not achieve at maximum doses of those medications\n\nSplit Dosing\nSome people split their weekly dose into two injections (for example, 1 mg twice per week instead of 2 mg once per week). This compresses the peak-to-trough ratio and keeps serum levels more stable. The total weekly dose drives steady state concentration, not injection frequency. So 2 mg once a week and 1 mg twice a week produce the same average steady-state concentration. Split dosing can help manage GI side effects and the peak-related skin sensitivity.\n\nThis is different from microdosing. In pharmacology, microdosing means less than 1/100th of the dose that produces a pharmacological effect. What most people describe as microdosing retatrutide is actually split dosing. Actual microdosing of retatrutide does not make sense because you would never reach a steady-state concentration high enough to produce meaningful results.\n\nReconstitution\nThe reconstitution depends on your vial size and desired concentration. Work with your specific vial to calculate the concentration based on total peptide content divided by the volume of bacteriostatic water added.","frequency":"Twice weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":4,"unit":"mg","mg":4},{"value":8,"unit":"mg","mg":8}],"strengths":[10]},{"name":"Retatrutide 20mg + Trizepatide 40mg (10ml)","protocolName":"Retatrutide","category":"Weight Loss / Metabolic","overview":"Retatrutide is a triple hormone receptor agonist developed by Eli Lilly that simultaneously activates three receptor systems: GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1), GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide), and glucagon. This triple action is what separates it from every other weight loss compound currently available or in development.\n\nTo put the differences in context, semaglutide activates one receptor (GLP-1) and produces roughly 15% average weight loss. Tirzepatide activates two receptors (GLP-1 and GIP) and produces roughly 20% average weight loss. Retatrutide activates all three and in Phase 2 trials produced 24.2% average weight loss at 48 weeks. Phase 3 data confirmed this, showing 28.7% average weight loss at 68 weeks at the highest dose.\n\nThe compound is administered as a once-weekly subcutaneous injection. It has a half-life of approximately 6 days, which supports that weekly dosing schedule.\n\nAs of early 2026, retatrutide is not FDA approved. It is currently in Phase 3 clinical trials through the TRIUMPH program, which enrolls over 5,800 participants across four studies. The first Phase 3 results from TRIUMPH-4 were announced in December 2025. FDA submission is planned for late 2026 or early 2027, with potential approval in late 2027.\n\nEli Lilly has pursued a biologics license application rather than a standard drug application, which would grant up to 12 years of market exclusivity versus 5 years. They are also building a $27 billion manufacturing facility in anticipation of demand.","schedule":"TITRATION SCHEDULE\nWeeks 1 to 4:      2 mg once weekly\nWeeks 5 to 8:      4 mg once weekly\nWeeks 9 to 12:     8 mg once weekly\nWeek 13 onward:    12 mg once weekly (maximum dose)\n\nIMPORTANT NOTES\nStarting at 2 mg instead of 4 mg makes a real difference in tolerability.\nMany people get excellent results at 8 mg without needing 12 mg.\nIf side effects are rough at any step, stay at that dose for another 4 weeks.\nThere is no benefit to pushing through severe nausea.\nTake your injection on the same day each week.\n\nNot Everyone Needs Maximum Dose\nConsider your actual goal:\n\nIf 15 to 20% weight loss achieves your target: 4 to 8 mg may be sufficient\n\nIf you need 20% or more: 8 to 12 mg may benefit you\n\nIf you previously used semaglutide or tirzepatide: retatrutide at moderate doses may produce results you could not achieve at maximum doses of those medications\n\nSplit Dosing\nSome people split their weekly dose into two injections (for example, 1 mg twice per week instead of 2 mg once per week). This compresses the peak-to-trough ratio and keeps serum levels more stable. The total weekly dose drives steady state concentration, not injection frequency. So 2 mg once a week and 1 mg twice a week produce the same average steady-state concentration. Split dosing can help manage GI side effects and the peak-related skin sensitivity.\n\nThis is different from microdosing. In pharmacology, microdosing means less than 1/100th of the dose that produces a pharmacological effect. What most people describe as microdosing retatrutide is actually split dosing. Actual microdosing of retatrutide does not make sense because you would never reach a steady-state concentration high enough to produce meaningful results.\n\nReconstitution\nThe reconstitution depends on your vial size and desired concentration. Work with your specific vial to calculate the concentration based on total peptide content divided by the volume of bacteriostatic water added.","frequency":"Twice weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":4,"unit":"mg","mg":4},{"value":8,"unit":"mg","mg":8}],"strengths":[60]},{"name":"Selank","protocolName":"Selank","category":"Cognitive / Nootropic / Sleep","overview":"Selank is a synthetic heptapeptide made up of seven amino acids (Thr-Lys-Pro-Arg-Pro-Gly-Pro) that reduces anxiety and sharpens cognitive function without causing sedation, tolerance, or dependency. Your body naturally produces a peptide called tuftsin, which is a small fragment of immunoglobulin G (your main antibody) that supports immune function. Russian scientists at the Institute of Molecular Genetics took the tuftsin sequence and added three amino acids (Pro-Gly-Pro) to the end of it, creating a more metabolically stable compound that can cross into the brain.\n\nThe result is a compound that has been approved as a prescription medication in Russia since 2009 for generalized anxiety disorder and neurasthenia. It is available there as a simple nasal spray called Selanc, and thousands of patients have used it clinically. It was also added to the Russian List of Vital and Essential Drugs.\n\nHere is what makes Selank different from conventional anti-anxiety drugs. Benzodiazepines like Xanax, Valium, and phenazepam work, but they sedate you, impair your memory, build tolerance, and create physical dependency. SSRIs take 4 to 6 weeks to build up and come with their own side effects including emotional blunting. In a clinical trial of 62 patients with generalized anxiety disorder, Selank produced anxiolytic effects comparable to medazepam (a benzodiazepine), but without the sedation, cognitive impairment, or addiction risk. It also had antiasthenic and psychostimulant effects that the benzodiazepine did not. You stay sharp and alert while feeling calmer.\n\nSelank is not FDA approved in the United States. It is available as a research chemical.","schedule":"Practical Protocols\n\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nINTRANASAL PROTOCOL\n\nDose:           200 to 400 mcg per administration\n\nFrequency:      2 to 3 times daily\n\nTotal daily:    400 to 1000 mcg\n\nTiming:         Morning and early afternoon preferred\n\nDuration:       10 to 14 days per cycle (Russian clinical protocol)\n\n4 to 6 weeks (extended community protocol)\n\nSUBCUTANEOUS INJECTION PROTOCOL\n\nDose:           100 to 300 mcg per day\n\nFrequency:      Once daily\n\nTiming:         Morning preferred\n\nDuration:       4 to 6 weeks\n\nCYCLE GUIDANCE\n\nShort cycle:    10 to 14 days for acute stress or specific events\n\nStandard cycle: 4 to 6 weeks for general anxiety or cognitive support\n\nBreak:          1 to 3 weeks between cycles recommended\n\nNo tapering required when stopping\n\nTiming Considerations\n\nMorning and early afternoon dosing works best for most people. Although Selank is not stimulating in the traditional sense, its effects on alertness and cognitive function mean that some users report difficulty winding down if they dose too late in the day.\n\nSelank does not need to be taken on an empty stomach. There are no fasting requirements, and it does not interact with food absorption.\n\nImportant Notes\n\nEffects build gradually for most users over 5 to 14 days. …","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":200,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.2},{"value":100,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.1}],"strengths":[5,10,30]},{"name":"Selank+Semax","protocolName":"Selank","category":"Cognitive / Nootropic / Sleep","overview":"Selank is a synthetic heptapeptide made up of seven amino acids (Thr-Lys-Pro-Arg-Pro-Gly-Pro) that reduces anxiety and sharpens cognitive function without causing sedation, tolerance, or dependency. Your body naturally produces a peptide called tuftsin, which is a small fragment of immunoglobulin G (your main antibody) that supports immune function. Russian scientists at the Institute of Molecular Genetics took the tuftsin sequence and added three amino acids (Pro-Gly-Pro) to the end of it, creating a more metabolically stable compound that can cross into the brain.\n\nThe result is a compound that has been approved as a prescription medication in Russia since 2009 for generalized anxiety disorder and neurasthenia. It is available there as a simple nasal spray called Selanc, and thousands of patients have used it clinically. It was also added to the Russian List of Vital and Essential Drugs.\n\nHere is what makes Selank different from conventional anti-anxiety drugs. Benzodiazepines like Xanax, Valium, and phenazepam work, but they sedate you, impair your memory, build tolerance, and create physical dependency. SSRIs take 4 to 6 weeks to build up and come with their own side effects including emotional blunting. In a clinical trial of 62 patients with generalized anxiety disorder, Selank produced anxiolytic effects comparable to medazepam (a benzodiazepine), but without the sedation, cognitive impairment, or addiction risk. It also had antiasthenic and psychostimulant effects that the benzodiazepine did not. You stay sharp and alert while feeling calmer.\n\nSelank is not FDA approved in the United States. It is available as a research chemical.","schedule":"Practical Protocols\n\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose-finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nINTRANASAL PROTOCOL\n\nDose:           200 to 400 mcg per administration\n\nFrequency:      2 to 3 times daily\n\nTotal daily:    400 to 1000 mcg\n\nTiming:         Morning and early afternoon preferred\n\nDuration:       10 to 14 days per cycle (Russian clinical protocol)\n\n4 to 6 weeks (extended community protocol)\n\nSUBCUTANEOUS INJECTION PROTOCOL\n\nDose:           100 to 300 mcg per day\n\nFrequency:      Once daily\n\nTiming:         Morning preferred\n\nDuration:       4 to 6 weeks\n\nCYCLE GUIDANCE\n\nShort cycle:    10 to 14 days for acute stress or specific events\n\nStandard cycle: 4 to 6 weeks for general anxiety or cognitive support\n\nBreak:          1 to 3 weeks between cycles recommended\n\nNo tapering required when stopping\n\nTiming Considerations\n\nMorning and early afternoon dosing works best for most people. Although Selank is not stimulating in the traditional sense, its effects on alertness and cognitive function mean that some users report difficulty winding down if they dose too late in the day.\n\nSelank does not need to be taken on an empty stomach. There are no fasting requirements, and it does not interact with food absorption.\n\nImportant Notes\n\nEffects build gradually for most users over 5 to 14 days. …","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":200,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.2},{"value":100,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.1}],"strengths":[10,20]},{"name":"Semaglutide","protocolName":"Semaglutide","category":"Weight Loss / Metabolic","overview":"Semaglutide is a GLP-1 receptor agonist, which means it mimics a hormone your gut already produces called glucagon-like peptide-1. Your body naturally releases GLP-1 from cells in your small intestine after you eat, and it plays a role in blood sugar regulation, appetite signaling, and gastric motility. Semaglutide is a synthetic version of that hormone, engineered to last much longer in your body so it can be dosed once per week.\n\nIt was first approved by the FDA in 2017 under the brand name Ozempic for type 2 diabetes management at doses up to 2.0 mg per week. In 2021, the FDA approved a higher-dose version at 2.4 mg per week under the brand name Wegovy for chronic weight management. In December 2025, the FDA approved an oral tablet version of Wegovy at 25 mg daily for weight management, making it the first oral GLP-1 agonist approved for obesity.\n\nSemaglutide has been studied in some of the largest and most rigorous clinical trials in obesity medicine. The STEP program includes multiple Phase 3 trials enrolling thousands of participants, and the SELECT cardiovascular outcomes trial enrolled over 17,000 people. This is a drug with an enormous amount of published human data behind it.\n\nThat said, semaglutide is a single-receptor agonist. It activates one receptor: GLP-1. Newer compounds like tirzepatide activate two receptors (GLP-1 and GIP), and retatrutide activates three (GLP-1, GIP, and glucagon). Understanding what semaglutide does and what it does not do at the receptor level is essential for understanding where it fits in the landscape of GLP-1 based therapies.","schedule":"STANDARD TITRATION (subcutaneous, weekly)\nWeeks 1 to 4:       0.25 mg (starting dose)\nWeeks 5 to 8:       0.5 mg\nWeeks 9 to 12:      1.0 mg\nWeeks 13 to 16:     1.7 mg\nWeek 17 onward:     2.4 mg (full weight loss maintenance dose)\n\nFOR TYPE 2 DIABETES (Ozempic)\nWeeks 1 to 4:       0.25 mg\nWeek 5 onward:      0.5 mg (can increase to 1.0 or 2.0 mg based on response)\n\nNot Everyone Needs the Maximum Dose\nThe 2.4 mg target is based on clinical trial protocols designed to show maximum effect. In practice, many people achieve excellent results at lower doses. Some people lose significant weight at 1.0 mg with minimal side effects. Pushing to 2.4 mg may add more side effects without proportional additional benefit. The optimal dose depends on how much weight you need to lose, how sensitive you are to the appetite suppression effects, how well you tolerate the gastrointestinal side effects, and whether you are using it primarily for metabolic health or for significant weight loss.\n\nImportant Notes\nDo not skip titration steps. Starting at higher doses causes severe nausea and vomiting. If side effects are strong at any step, stay at that dose for an additional 4 weeks before increasing. Take your injection on the same day each week. You can change the day as long as your last dose was at least 2 days prior.\n\nRECONSTITUTION (research grade lyophilized powder)\nVial size:      Varies (5 mg, 10 mg, 20 mg, 30 mg)\nBac water:      Adjust volume to achieve desired concentration\nStorage:        Refrigerate after reconstitution","frequency":"Weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":0.25,"unit":"mg","mg":0.25},{"value":0.5,"unit":"mg","mg":0.5},{"value":1,"unit":"mg","mg":1},{"value":1.7,"unit":"mg","mg":1.7},{"value":2.4,"unit":"mg","mg":2.4}],"strengths":[5,10,15,20,30,50]},{"name":"Semax","protocolName":"Semax","category":"Cognitive / Nootropic / Sleep","overview":"Semax is a synthetic peptide made up of seven amino acids that was developed in Russia in 1982 at the Institute of Molecular Genetics. It was originally created to help people recovering from strokes and brain injuries, and it has been used as a prescription medication in Russia for over three decades. Doctors there prescribe it for stroke recovery, traumatic brain injury, cognitive decline, and optic nerve disease.\n\nWhat makes Semax different from stimulants like Adderall or caffeine is how it works. It does not just rev you up temporarily. It actually increases brain derived neurotrophic factor, which is a protein your brain uses to grow new neurons, form memories, and adapt to challenges. Think of BDNF like fertilizer for your brain. When BDNF levels are high, you learn faster, your memory works better, and your brain is more resilient to stress and damage. When BDNF levels are low, that is associated with depression, cognitive decline, and neurodegenerative diseases.\n\nSemax comes from a fragment of ACTH, which stands for adrenocorticotropic hormone. ACTH is produced by your pituitary gland and its main job is telling your adrenal glands to release cortisol. Semax only uses amino acids 4 through 10 of that hormone. Because it is just that fragment, it does not have any effect on your cortisol levels. Scientists also modified it with a specific amino acid sequence called Pro-Gly-Pro that makes it more stable and allows it to cross into your brain more effectively. Think of it like taking the governor off the version your body already makes.\n\nThe peptide is typically taken intranasally as a nasal spray or drops, which allows it to cross the blood brain barrier directly through the nasal passages. You can also inject it subcutaneously for more precise dosing. …","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are not derived from published dose finding studies. They represent clinical practice patterns and community consensus.\n\nINTRANASAL PROTOCOL (standard)\nDose:           200 to 600 mcg per day\nFrequency:      Once or twice daily\nTiming:         Morning or early afternoon (avoid late day dosing)\nAdministration: Intranasal spray or drops\nDuration:       10 to 14 days on, then 2 to 4 weeks off\nCycle:          Repeat as needed\n\nSUBCUTANEOUS PROTOCOL\nDose:           200 to 600 mcg per day\nFrequency:      Once daily\nTiming:         Morning\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection\nDuration:       10 to 14 days on, then 2 to 4 weeks off\n\nNASAL SPRAY PREPARATION\nVial size:      10 mg\nSterile saline: 1 mL (use saline, not bac water, for nasal use)\nConcentration:  200 mcg per spray (standard nasal sprayer delivers ~0.1 mL)\nYield:          50 sprays per vial\n\nBY APPLICATION\nCognitive support:        200 to 400 mcg daily\nNeuroprotection:          400 to 600 mcg daily\nPost-injury recovery:     600 mcg daily (clinical studies used up to 6,000 mcg)\n\nWhy You Need to Cycle\nSemax works on BDNF pathways. If you are constantly stimulating those pathways, they adapt and you get diminishing returns over time. The standard approach is 10 to 14 days on, then a break of 2 to 4 weeks. This is not something you run year round without breaks.\n\nTiming\nTake Semax early morning or early afternoon. Some people report that it affects sleep if taken too late in the day. This makes sense given that it activates dopaminergic pathways and increases brain activity.","frequency":"Twice daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":200,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.2}],"strengths":[5,10,30]},{"name":"Sermorelin Acetate","protocolName":"Sermorelin","category":"Growth Hormone Family","overview":"Sermorelin is a synthetic version of growth hormone releasing hormone, commonly abbreviated GHRH. Specifically, it consists of the first 29 amino acids of the full 44 amino acid GHRH molecule that your hypothalamus naturally produces. This truncated version retains full biological activity, meaning it activates the same receptor just as effectively as the complete hormone for stimulating growth hormone release from your pituitary gland.\n\nAlso known as GHRH (1-29) or GRF (1-29), Sermorelin was approved by the FDA in 1997 for treating children with growth hormone deficiency under the brand name Geref. In 2008, the manufacturer discontinued production for commercial reasons, not because of safety concerns. It remains available through compounding pharmacies for off-label use.\n\nSermorelin is the original GHRH analog. It came before CJC-1295, before tesamorelin, and before the modifications that made those newer versions more resistant to enzymatic breakdown. That means Sermorelin is the most \"natural\" version in the sense that it most closely resembles the signal your body already makes. But that also means it is the most vulnerable to degradation. The enzyme DPP-4, which stands for dipeptidyl aminopeptidase 4, specifically recognizes the Tyr-Ala sequence at the front end of the Sermorelin chain and cuts it apart. This gives Sermorelin a very short half-life of about 10 to 20 minutes, which is significantly shorter than what you get from the modified analogs.\n\nWhat Sermorelin preserves that matters is the pulsatile release pattern. Because it clears quickly, the growth hormone response you get from Sermorelin looks a lot like the sharp pulses your body produces on its own. …","schedule":"Standard Protocol\n\nDose:           200 to 500 mcg per injection\n\nFrequency:      Once daily\n\nTiming:         30 minutes before bed, on an empty stomach\n\nSchedule:       Daily\n\nCycle Length:   3 to 6 months\n\nTake on an empty stomach, at least 2 hours after eating. Food, especially carbohydrates, raises insulin and blunts the growth hormone response. Bedtime dosing is preferred because it aligns with your body's largest natural growth hormone pulse, which occurs during early deep sleep.\n\nDosing by Goal\n\nGeneral wellness / anti-aging:    200 to 300 mcg before bed\n\nPerformance / recovery:           300 to 500 mcg before bed\n\nMaximum effect (twice daily):     300 mcg morning (fasted) + 300 mcg before bed\n\nCombined With Ipamorelin\n\nSermorelin:     200 to 300 mcg\n\nIpamorelin:     100 to 200 mcg\n\nTiming:         Pinned together before bed, fasted\n\nFrequency:      Daily\n\nCycle Length:   8 to 12 weeks\n\nCombining a GHRH peptide like Sermorelin with a GHRP like ipamorelin produces significantly more growth hormone than either one alone. Research shows the synergistic effect can produce 77 to 225 percent greater growth hormone release compared to either compound used individually. They work through different receptor pathways and amplify each other. GHRH increases the number of pituitary cells releasing growth hormone. Ghrelin agonists increase the amount released per cell and suppress somatostatin, removing the brake on the system.\n\nReconstitution\n\nVial size:         5 mg\n\nBac water:         2 mL\n\nConcentration:     250 mcg per 10 units on an insulin syringe\n\nStandard dose:     300 mcg = 12 units\n\nWhere to Inject\n\nSubcutaneous injection, typically in the abdomen or thigh. Rotate injection sites to prevent scar tissue buildup.\n\nStorage and Handling\n\nBefore Reconstitution\n\nStore lyophilized (powder) vials in the refrigerator at 36 to 46 degrees Fahrenheit (2 to 8 degrees Celsius). Can also be stored in the freezer for longer periods. Protect from light.\n\nAfter Reconstitution\n\nStore in the refrigerator at 36 to 46 degrees Fahrenheit (2 to 8 degrees Celsius). This is standard peptide storage. …","frequency":"Twice daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":200,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.2}],"strengths":[2,5,10,15]},{"name":"SLU-PP-332","protocolName":"SLU-PP-332","category":"Weight Loss / Metabolic","overview":"SLU-PP-332 is not a peptide. Peptides are chains of amino acids. SLU-PP-332 is a synthetic small molecule with a molecular weight of 290.32 g/mol and the chemical formula C18H14N2O2. It is included in this course because it is commonly sold alongside peptides by research chemical vendors and my community asks about it constantly. If you see companies marketing this as a peptide, that tells you something about the quality of information in this space.\n\nThe compound was developed at Saint Louis University in the early 2000s. It activates a set of receptors called estrogen related receptors, abbreviated ERRs. These receptors control how your cells produce energy, build mitochondria, and burn fat for fuel.\n\nThe name estrogen related receptors causes confusion. These receptors have nothing to do with estrogen. They are called orphan receptors because when scientists discovered them, they did not know what naturally activated them. Scientists named them estrogen related receptors because they look similar in structure to estrogen receptors. ERRs do not bind to estrogen and activating them has no effect on your estrogen levels.\n\nThere are three types of these receptors: ERR alpha, ERR beta, and ERR gamma. SLU-PP-332 activates all three, which is why researchers call it a panagonist. The compound is most potent at ERR alpha.\n\nSLU-PP-332 is not FDA approved. It has never been tested in humans in any clinical trial. Everything we know about how this compound works in a living organism comes from mouse studies. A 2025 pilot study used SLU-PP-332 on human muscle cells in a lab dish, but that is not the same as giving it to a person. Anyone using this compound is participating in an uncontrolled human experiment. That is the literal reality of the situation. …","schedule":"This section is provided for educational context only. I do not recommend using SLU-PP-332 due to the significant issues outlined below. No human dosing protocol has been established by any clinical trial.\n\nWhat the Mouse Studies Used\nEvery published study administered SLU-PP-332 by intraperitoneal injection using a DMSO-based vehicle. The doses ranged from 25 to 50 mg/kg daily.\n\nExercise study:      50 mg/kg twice daily IP (7 to 15 days)\nFat loss study:      50 mg/kg twice daily IP (28 days)\nKidney study:        25 mg/kg daily IP (8 weeks)\nHeart failure study: 25 mg/kg twice daily IP (duration not specified)\n\nHuman Equivalent Dose Calculation\nIf you convert the mouse doses using standard allometric scaling (dividing by 12.3), the human equivalent dose comes out to approximately:\n\n25 mg/kg mouse dose      = ~140 mg/day for a 154 lb adult\n50 mg/kg twice daily     = ~560 mg/day for a 154 lb adult\n\nThese are estimates using standard FDA conversion guidelines. They assume 100% bioavailability from intraperitoneal injection. Oral bioavailability is unknown and likely far lower.\n\nWhat Is Actually Being Sold\nProducts on the market contain anywhere from 100 mcg to a few milligrams per capsule. There is a massive gap between what produced effects in mice and what is commercially available. Even if you could absorb oral SLU-PP-332 consistently, the doses being sold are a tiny fraction of what the research used.\n\nThe Oral Bioavailability Problem\nSLU-PP-332 is hydrophobic. It does not dissolve in water. It is insoluble in water, sparingly soluble in DMSO (58 mg/mL), and only slightly soluble in ethanol (2 mg/mL).\n\nYour intestinal wall has a water layer that is about 100 micrometers thick. Compounds have to dissolve and pass through that layer to get absorbed into your body. When you take a dry powder capsule of something that does not dissolve in water, absorption becomes unpredictable. It depends on your bile secretion, what you ate, individual differences in your gut, and other factors. Some people might absorb a reasonable amount. Others absorb almost nothing.\n\nThis explains the mixed reviews. The compound may work, but people are not absorbing it consistently. …","frequency":"Twice daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":25,"unit":"mg","mg":25}],"strengths":[5]},{"name":"SS-31","protocolName":"SS-31","category":"Mitochondrial / Longevity","overview":"SS-31 is a synthetic peptide made of four amino acids that targets the inner membrane of your mitochondria, which is where your body produces energy. Its technical name is elamipretide, and it was developed by Dr. Hazel Szeto at Weill Cornell Medical College and Dr. Peter Schiller at the Montreal Clinical Research Institute. The name SS-31 stands for Szeto-Schiller peptide 31.\n\nOn September 19, 2025, elamipretide received accelerated approval from the FDA under the brand name Forzinity for treating Barth syndrome, an extremely rare genetic disorder affecting about 150 people in the United States. This makes SS-31 the first FDA approved treatment that specifically targets mitochondria, and the first mitochondria-targeted peptide to receive FDA approval for any indication.\n\nWhat makes SS-31 fundamentally different from other mitochondrial compounds is what it does. NAD+ provides fuel. MOTS-c activates pathways. SS-31 repairs the structural damage that prevents your mitochondria from working properly in the first place. If MOTS-c is the supercharger and NAD+ is the fuel, SS-31 is the mechanic. It is fixing structural damage, cracks, leaks, broken components. Using fuel and upgrades on a damaged engine gives you limited results. You have to fix the damage first.\n\nThis is exactly why I say for folks over 35 to 40 who have never done peptides before and are starting on their health journey, absolutely start with SS-31 first to repair the problem before trying to optimize it.","schedule":"Practical Protocol for Age-Related Decline\nFor otherwise healthy individuals experiencing age-related mitochondrial decline:\n\nREPAIR PROTOCOL\nDose:           1 to 2 mg per day\nFrequency:      Once daily\nTiming:         No fasting required, no specific timing constraints\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection\nDuration:       4 to 8 weeks (repair phase)\n\nRECONSTITUTION (10 mg vial)\nBac water:      2 mL\nConcentration:  500 mcg per 10 units on insulin syringe\n1 mg per 20 units on insulin syringe\n\nProtocol Sequencing (Repair First Approach)\nFor people over 35 to 55 with declining energy, slower recovery, and metabolic issues:\n\nWeeks 1 through 4 (or up to 8): Repair Phase SS-31 at 1 to 2 mg daily plus NAD+ at 100 mg subcutaneously three times per week. You are repairing accumulated mitochondrial damage while giving your cells the extra fuel they need to optimize energy.\n\nWeeks 5 through 12: Optimize Phase Replace SS-31 with MOTS-c. Start with 10 mg split into three injections per week (Monday, Wednesday, Friday). Continue NAD+ at 100 mg three times per week. Now you are optimizing the repaired mitochondria.\n\nAfter the 12-week cycle: Take 4 to 8 weeks completely off. Then you can repeat this protocol 2 to 3 times per year.\n\nPrevention First Protocol\nFor younger or healthier individuals who want preventative optimization:\n\nStart with MOTS-c at 10 mg split into three injections per week (Monday, Wednesday, Friday) plus NAD+ at 100 mg three times per week. At weeks 4 through 8, assess whether you still have significant fatigue, poor recovery, or metabolic issues. If yes, add SS-31 at 1 to 2 mg daily for 4 to 8 weeks while continuing MOTS-c and NAD+. If not, continue with just MOTS-c and NAD+ for maintenance.\n\nA Note on Higher Doses\nYou may see dosages recommended online as high as 5 mg, 10 mg, or even 50 mg per day. …","frequency":"Weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":1,"unit":"mg","mg":1}],"strengths":[10,50]},{"name":"TB500","protocolName":"TB-500","category":"Recovery / Repair","overview":"TB-500 is a synthetic version of thymosin beta-4, a naturally occurring 43 amino acid protein found in nearly every human and animal cell. The full protein weighs about 5 kilodaltons, making it small enough to travel through tissues with relative ease. Your body produces thymosin beta-4 in high concentrations in platelets, white blood cells, plasma, and wound fluid. It is present in virtually all tissue types except red blood cells. The protein plays essential roles in cell migration, blood vessel formation, inflammation control, and tissue repair.\n\nThymosin beta-4 was originally discovered in the thymus gland during immune function research in the 1960s by Allan Goldstein and colleagues. Since then, it has been studied extensively for wound healing, cardiac repair, neuroprotection, and tissue regeneration. The World Health Organization has assigned it the International Nonproprietary Name \"timbetasin.\"\n\nHere is the important distinction to make upfront. TB-500 is not FDA approved for human use. In 2023, the FDA classified it as a Category 2 bulk drug substance, effectively prohibiting its use in compounded medications. The World Anti-Doping Agency has banned it under the S0 Unapproved Substances category. It has been at the center of major doping controversies in professional sports, including suspensions of players from the Essendon Football Club in Australian rules football and the Cronulla-Sutherland Sharks rugby league team.","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nWOLVERINE STACK PROTOCOL (TB-500 + BPC-157, daily dosing)\nTB-500:         250 to 500 mcg per day\nBPC-157:        250 to 500 mcg per day\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection\nTiming:         Any time of day (no fasting required)\nDuration:       4 to 8 weeks for active healing\n\nTRADITIONAL TB-500 PROTOCOL (twice weekly loading)\nLoading Phase:\nDose:         2 to 2.5 mg twice weekly\nTotal:        4 to 5 mg per week\nDuration:     4 to 6 weeks\nSchedule:     Monday/Thursday or Tuesday/Friday\n\nMaintenance Phase:\nDose:         2 to 2.5 mg once every 1 to 2 weeks\nDuration:     As needed for ongoing recovery\n\nBY APPLICATION\nAcute injury recovery:     Loading at 4 to 5 mg per week for 4 to 6 weeks\nChronic injury support:    Loading at 4 to 5 mg per week for 6 to 8 weeks\nPost-surgical recovery:    Loading at 4 to 6 mg per week for 4 to 6 weeks (with surgeon approval)\nGeneral maintenance:       2 to 2.5 mg once every 2 weeks\n\nRECONSTITUTION (standard, when used as daily Wolverine Stack)\nVial size:      5 mg\nBac water:      2 mL\nConcentration:  250 mcg per 10 units on insulin syringe\n\nWhere to Inject\nTB-500 works systemically. It distributes throughout your body regardless of where you inject it because of its low molecular weight and ability to travel through tissues without binding to the extracellular matrix. You do not need to inject near the injury site. Subcutaneous injection into the abdominal area or thigh is most common.\n\nThis is a key difference from BPC-157. …","frequency":"Twice weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":2,"unit":"mg","mg":2}],"strengths":[2,5,10,20]},{"name":"Tesamorelin","protocolName":"Tesamorelin","category":"Growth Hormone Family","overview":"Tesamorelin is a synthetic form of growth hormone releasing hormone, commonly abbreviated GHRH. It is a 44 amino acid peptide that matches the full sequence of your body's natural GHRH molecule, with one important modification: a trans-3-hexenoic acid group is attached to the tyrosine at the front end of the chain. This modification protects the peptide from being broken down by an enzyme called DPP-4, which is the primary reason natural GHRH only lasts about two minutes in your bloodstream before it gets chopped apart.\n\nTesamorelin was developed by Theratechnologies, Inc. of Canada and approved in 2010 under the brand name Egrifta for a specific clinical use: reducing excess abdominal fat in adults with HIV who have lipodystrophy. An updated formulation called Egrifta WR was approved in 2024, allowing weekly reconstitution instead of daily. It is now being used off label for body recomposition and anti-aging purposes.\n\nHere is an important distinction that I want you to understand from the start. Tesamorelin gets called \"the visceral fat peptide,\" but it is not a peptide that specifically targets visceral fat. That is a misconception. The reason it has that reputation is because the clinical trials specifically measured visceral fat reduction using CT scans, and that is the endpoint that earned it the approval. The mechanism is the same as CJC-1295 and Sermorelin. Same pathway, same receptor, same downstream cascade. The difference is in what was measured, not in what the peptide does. If CJC-1295 had been put through a 26-week visceral fat study with CT scans, we would probably have very similar data. But that study was never funded because the company behind CJC was not pursuing an approval for HIV lipodystrophy. …","schedule":"Standard Protocol\nDose:           2 mg (2000 mcg) per day\nFrequency:      Once daily, subcutaneous injection\nTiming:         30 to 60 minutes before bed, fasted\nOR fasted in the morning, at least 2 hours before eating\nCycle Length:   Clinical trials used continuous daily dosing for 26 weeks minimum\n\nThe 2 mg daily dose comes directly from the FDA-approved prescribing information and is the dose used across all published clinical trials. No published dose-finding studies support lower doses for efficacy in visceral fat reduction.\n\nCommon Off-Label Protocols\n3 to 6 months on, then 1 to 2 months off to give your system a break. Some anti-aging practitioners have patients on continuous lower doses, but that approach is less studied.\n\nCombined Protocol With Ipamorelin\nTesamorelin:    1 to 2 mg\nIpamorelin:     100 to 200 mcg\nTiming:         Pinned together before bed, fasted\nFrequency:      Daily\nCycle Length:   60 to 90 days\n\nThe synergy between GHRH and GHRP only happens when both pathways are activated in the same window. GHRP suppresses somatostatin while GHRH drives pituitary output, and that combined signal produces a much larger growth hormone pulse than either one alone. Splitting them apart just gives two smaller separate pulses, which defeats the purpose of stacking.\n\nWhy Fasted Timing Matters\nWhen you inject fasted before bed, you have low insulin and high ghrelin. This maximizes your growth hormone pulse. That growth hormone circulates for 2 to 3 hours overnight, mobilizing fat while you sleep. When you wake up and eat breakfast, insulin rises and your liver converts that circulating growth hormone into IGF-1. That IGF-1 works for you all day for recovery, collagen production, and metabolic benefits.\n\nSome people experience sleep disruption with nighttime dosing. If that happens, switch to morning dosing: inject fasted, wait at least 2 hours before eating. Both approaches are effective.\n\nReconstitution\nVial size:         5 mg\nBac water:         1 mL\nConcentration:     5000 mcg per mL (5 mg per mL)\nStandard dose:     2 mg = 40 units on an insulin syringe\nVial duration:     2.5 days at the standard 2 mg daily dose\n\nWhere to Inject …","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":1,"unit":"mg","mg":1},{"value":100,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.1}],"strengths":[2,5,10,15,20]},{"name":"Tesamorelin + Ipamorelin","protocolName":"Tesamorelin","category":"Growth Hormone Family","overview":"Tesamorelin is a synthetic form of growth hormone releasing hormone, commonly abbreviated GHRH. It is a 44 amino acid peptide that matches the full sequence of your body's natural GHRH molecule, with one important modification: a trans-3-hexenoic acid group is attached to the tyrosine at the front end of the chain. This modification protects the peptide from being broken down by an enzyme called DPP-4, which is the primary reason natural GHRH only lasts about two minutes in your bloodstream before it gets chopped apart.\n\nTesamorelin was developed by Theratechnologies, Inc. of Canada and approved in 2010 under the brand name Egrifta for a specific clinical use: reducing excess abdominal fat in adults with HIV who have lipodystrophy. An updated formulation called Egrifta WR was approved in 2024, allowing weekly reconstitution instead of daily. It is now being used off label for body recomposition and anti-aging purposes.\n\nHere is an important distinction that I want you to understand from the start. Tesamorelin gets called \"the visceral fat peptide,\" but it is not a peptide that specifically targets visceral fat. That is a misconception. The reason it has that reputation is because the clinical trials specifically measured visceral fat reduction using CT scans, and that is the endpoint that earned it the approval. The mechanism is the same as CJC-1295 and Sermorelin. Same pathway, same receptor, same downstream cascade. The difference is in what was measured, not in what the peptide does. If CJC-1295 had been put through a 26-week visceral fat study with CT scans, we would probably have very similar data. But that study was never funded because the company behind CJC was not pursuing an approval for HIV lipodystrophy. …","schedule":"Standard Protocol\nDose:           2 mg (2000 mcg) per day\nFrequency:      Once daily, subcutaneous injection\nTiming:         30 to 60 minutes before bed, fasted\nOR fasted in the morning, at least 2 hours before eating\nCycle Length:   Clinical trials used continuous daily dosing for 26 weeks minimum\n\nThe 2 mg daily dose comes directly from the FDA-approved prescribing information and is the dose used across all published clinical trials. No published dose-finding studies support lower doses for efficacy in visceral fat reduction.\n\nCommon Off-Label Protocols\n3 to 6 months on, then 1 to 2 months off to give your system a break. Some anti-aging practitioners have patients on continuous lower doses, but that approach is less studied.\n\nCombined Protocol With Ipamorelin\nTesamorelin:    1 to 2 mg\nIpamorelin:     100 to 200 mcg\nTiming:         Pinned together before bed, fasted\nFrequency:      Daily\nCycle Length:   60 to 90 days\n\nThe synergy between GHRH and GHRP only happens when both pathways are activated in the same window. GHRP suppresses somatostatin while GHRH drives pituitary output, and that combined signal produces a much larger growth hormone pulse than either one alone. Splitting them apart just gives two smaller separate pulses, which defeats the purpose of stacking.\n\nWhy Fasted Timing Matters\nWhen you inject fasted before bed, you have low insulin and high ghrelin. This maximizes your growth hormone pulse. That growth hormone circulates for 2 to 3 hours overnight, mobilizing fat while you sleep. When you wake up and eat breakfast, insulin rises and your liver converts that circulating growth hormone into IGF-1. That IGF-1 works for you all day for recovery, collagen production, and metabolic benefits.\n\nSome people experience sleep disruption with nighttime dosing. If that happens, switch to morning dosing: inject fasted, wait at least 2 hours before eating. Both approaches are effective.\n\nReconstitution\nVial size:         5 mg\nBac water:         1 mL\nConcentration:     5000 mcg per mL (5 mg per mL)\nStandard dose:     2 mg = 40 units on an insulin syringe\nVial duration:     2.5 days at the standard 2 mg daily dose\n\nWhere to Inject …","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":1,"unit":"mg","mg":1},{"value":100,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.1}],"strengths":[10,20]},{"name":"Thymalin/Thymulin","protocolName":"Thymalin","category":"Immune / Inflammation","overview":"Thymalin is a complex of several short peptides extracted from the thymus glands of young calves. It was developed in Russia in the early 1980s by Vladimir Khavinson and Vyacheslav Morozov at the St. Petersburg Institute of Bioregulation and Gerontology. It has been approved for medical use in Russia since 1982.\n\nTo understand why Thymalin matters, you need to understand the thymus. The thymus is a small organ behind your breastbone where your T cells go to school. T cells are the backbone of your adaptive immune system. They identify infected cells, coordinate immune responses, and kill threats. The thymus is where they learn to do all of that.\n\nHere is the problem. Your thymus starts shrinking early in life, a process called thymic involution. By the time you reach your 40s, the functional tissue in your thymus has been largely replaced by fat. Your thymus is still there, but it is producing far fewer new T cells than it did when you were younger. This is one of the major reasons your immune system weakens with age. You get sick more often, recover slower, respond less effectively to vaccines, and become more vulnerable to infections and cancer.\n\nThymalin is not a single peptide with a single amino acid sequence like BPC-157 or Thymosin Alpha-1. It is a mixture of short peptides, with three bioactive components identified as key drivers of its effects: the dipeptides EW (glutamic acid and tryptophan) and KE (lysine and glutamic acid), and the tripeptide EDP (glutamic acid, aspartic acid, and proline). These short peptides work by binding directly to DNA and histone proteins to regulate gene expression related to immune function.\n\nThymalin has an unusual body of research behind it. …","schedule":"Practical Protocols\nThe following protocols are based on the dosing used in published clinical studies and clinical practice patterns. They are not derived from dose-finding optimization studies.\n\nSTANDARD PROTOCOL\nDose:           10 mg daily\nRoute:          Subcutaneous or intramuscular injection\nDuration:       10 consecutive days\nFrequency:      1 to 2 cycles per year\nCourse total:   100 mg per cycle (10 vials)\n\nBY APPLICATION\nImmune restoration:       10 mg daily for 10 days, repeat every 6 to 12 months\nAnti-aging/geroprotection: 10 mg daily for 10 days, annual or twice-yearly cycles\nPost-illness recovery:    5 to 10 mg daily for 5 to 10 days, begin after acute phase\nSeasonal prevention:      5 to 10 mg daily for 5 to 10 days, before cold and flu season\n\nRECONSTITUTION (standard)\nVial size:      10 mg\nBac water:      1 mL\nConcentration:  10 mg per mL\nFull dose:      Draw the entire 1 mL for a 10 mg dose\nHalf dose:      Draw 0.5 mL for a 5 mg dose\n\nAdministration Notes\nThymalin is typically administered once daily, preferably in the morning. Unlike most peptides that require ongoing daily use for weeks or months, Thymalin is given in short intensive courses followed by extended breaks of months between cycles. This pulsing approach is based on the protocols used in the published clinical studies.\n\nAt the standard 10 mg daily dose, each vial provides one day of use. A full 10-day course requires 10 vials. At the 5 mg daily dose, each vial provides two days of use. Reconstituted Thymalin should be refrigerated at 2 to 8 degrees Celsius and used within the course duration.\n\nThe original clinical research used intramuscular injection. Community practice has largely shifted to subcutaneous injection for ease of self-administration, at the same doses. …","frequency":"Daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":5,"unit":"mg","mg":5}],"strengths":[10,20]},{"name":"Thymosin Alpha-1","protocolName":"Thymosin Alpha-1","category":"Immune / Inflammation","overview":"Thymosin Alpha-1 is a 28 amino acid peptide that your thymus gland naturally produces to train and regulate your immune system. It has a molecular weight of 3,108 daltons and is derived from a larger precursor protein called prothymosin alpha. The synthetic version is called thymalfasin, sold under the brand name Zadaxin.\n\nYour thymus is the organ behind your breastbone where T cells learn how to do their job. T cells are your immune system's primary soldiers. They fight infections, kill virus-infected cells, and destroy abnormal cells before they become problems. Thymosin Alpha-1 is the specific signal the thymus uses to mature these T cells from raw recruits into fully trained responders.\n\nHere is why this matters as you age. Your thymus starts shrinking after puberty. By the time you are in your 40s and 50s, a significant portion of the functional tissue has been replaced by fat. This process is called thymic involution, and it is one of the primary reasons your immune system weakens as you get older. You produce fewer new T cells, the ones you have become less effective, and your ability to respond to new threats declines. That is immunosenescence, and it is why older adults are more susceptible to infections, respond poorly to vaccines, and have higher rates of cancer.\n\nThymosin Alpha-1 has been approved in over 35 countries for treating chronic hepatitis B, chronic hepatitis C, and as an adjunct therapy for certain cancers. It is one of the most studied immune peptides in existence, with over 11,000 human subjects enrolled across more than 30 clinical trials.\n\nWhat makes Thymosin Alpha-1 fundamentally different from typical immune supplements is that it modulates rather than just stimulates. If your immune system is underperforming, it brings it up. …","schedule":"STANDARD PROTOCOL\nDose:           1.6 mg (1,600 mcg) per injection\nFrequency:      Twice weekly (commonly Monday and Thursday)\nTiming:         No fasting required, no time-of-day restriction\nAdministration: Subcutaneous injection\nDuration:       4 to 8 weeks for general immune support\n\nBy Application\nGENERAL IMMUNE SUPPORT\nDose:           1.6 mg twice weekly\nDuration:       4 to 8 weeks\nCycling:        4 weeks off between cycles, or run seasonally\n\nCHRONIC INFECTION SUPPORT\nDose:           1.6 mg twice weekly\nDuration:       6 to 12 months (based on clinical trial durations)\nNote:           Often combined with other therapies\nDuration depends on clinical response\nPhysician supervision required\n\nVACCINE ENHANCEMENT\nDose:           1.6 mg twice weekly\nStart:          2 weeks before vaccination\nContinue:       2 to 4 weeks after vaccination\n\nReconstitution\nRECONSTITUTION (standard)\nVial size:      10 mg\nBac water:      2 mL\nConcentration:  250 mcg per 5 units on insulin syringe (0.05 mL)\n1.6 mg dose:    Draw 32 units (0.32 mL)\n\nWhere to Inject\nThymosin Alpha-1 is a systemic immune modulator. It does not need to be injected at a specific site. Subcutaneous injection in the abdomen, thigh, or upper arm all work equally well. Rotate injection sites to avoid irritation.","frequency":"Twice weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":1.6,"unit":"mg","mg":1.6}],"strengths":[2,5,10]},{"name":"Tirzepatide","protocolName":"Tirzepatide","category":"Weight Loss / Metabolic","overview":"Tirzepatide is a dual receptor agonist that activates both GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) and GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide) receptors at the same time. It is a 39-amino-acid synthetic peptide based on the native GIP sequence, modified with a C20 fatty diacid chain that binds to albumin in the blood and extends the half-life to approximately 5 days. That extended half-life is what allows once-weekly dosing.\n\nIt is FDA approved under two brand names. Mounjaro is approved for type 2 diabetes. Zepbound is approved for chronic weight management in adults with obesity or overweight with at least one weight-related condition, and also for moderate-to-severe obstructive sleep apnea in adults with obesity. Both are the same drug made by Eli Lilly, just with different labels and different insurance pathways.\n\nWhat makes tirzepatide significant is the dual receptor activation. Before tirzepatide, the only incretin-based drugs available targeted GLP-1 alone, like semaglutide. Researchers originally thought blocking GIP would help with weight loss. It turned out they had it backwards. Activating GIP alongside GLP-1 produces better results than GLP-1 alone, and the clinical trial data supports that clearly.\n\nTirzepatide is also being studied for metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH, formerly called NASH), heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, and other metabolic conditions. The research pipeline is expanding rapidly.","schedule":"Practical Protocol\nThe following titration matches the FDA-approved labeling and clinical trial design. Subcutaneous injection, once weekly, same day each week.\n\nSTANDARD TITRATION\nWeeks 1 to 4:      2.5 mg\nWeeks 5 to 8:      5 mg\nWeeks 9 to 12:     7.5 mg\nWeeks 13 to 16:    10 mg\nWeeks 17 to 20:    12.5 mg\nWeek 21 onward:    15 mg (maximum dose)\n\nUnderstanding the Titration\nTaking a higher dose does not mean you get better results. The results come from sustained time on the drug. The reason you increase the dose is because your body builds tolerance over time. The dose that was suppressing your appetite in month one might not work as well in month three. The titration is about maintaining consistent effectiveness, not chasing a bigger number.\n\nThe 2.5 mg starting dose is intentionally low to let your body adapt. Do not skip it.\n\nFinding Your Maintenance Dose\nNot everyone needs to reach 15 mg. The trial data shows that 10 mg produces nearly 20% weight loss on average, which is more than many people need.\n\nAt each dose level, assess three things. Are you losing weight at a satisfactory rate? Are side effects manageable? Is your appetite appropriately suppressed?\n\nIf you are losing 1 to 2 pounds per week with tolerable side effects, you may not need to increase further. The goal is minimum effective dose: the dose where you are getting the results you want with the side effects you can manage.\n\nIf side effects are difficult at any dose, you have three options. …","frequency":"Weekly","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":2.5,"unit":"mg","mg":2.5},{"value":5,"unit":"mg","mg":5},{"value":7.5,"unit":"mg","mg":7.5},{"value":10,"unit":"mg","mg":10},{"value":12.5,"unit":"mg","mg":12.5},{"value":15,"unit":"mg","mg":15}],"strengths":[5,10,15,20,30,40,45,50,60,70,80,90,100,120]},{"name":"VIP","protocolName":"VIP","category":"Immune / Inflammation","overview":"Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide, or VIP, is a 28 amino acid peptide hormone produced throughout your body, particularly in the gut, pancreas, and central nervous system. Despite its name suggesting it only affects the intestines, VIP has widespread effects on blood vessels, the immune system, circadian rhythms, and multiple organ systems.\n\nYour body naturally produces VIP, and it plays a crucial role in regulating inflammation, blood vessel dilation, smooth muscle relaxation, and communication between your nervous and immune systems. VIP is part of what scientists call the neuroendocrine immune network, a system where your nervous system and immune system communicate bidirectionally through shared chemical messengers.\n\nVIP has gained significant attention in functional medicine for its role in treating Chronic Inflammatory Response Syndrome (CIRS), a complex multi-system illness often triggered by exposure to water-damaged buildings and mold. Dr. Ritchie Shoemaker pioneered the use of VIP nasal spray as the final step in his CIRS treatment protocol, documenting its effects in over 10,000 patients. One of the hallmark findings in CIRS is abnormally low VIP levels, which contributes to the chronic inflammatory state and the wide range of symptoms these patients experience.\n\nThe peptide works through two main receptors (VPAC1 and VPAC2) found throughout the body. When VIP binds to these receptors, it triggers cellular cascades that reduce inflammation, promote tissue repair, and help restore normal function to systems disrupted by chronic inflammation.\n\nVIP is typically administered as a nasal spray, allowing direct delivery to the brain through the olfactory pathway and rapid systemic absorption. …","schedule":"Practical Protocols\n\nThe following protocols are based on Dr. Shoemaker's published protocol and clinical practice patterns.\n\nSTANDARD CIRS PROTOCOL (Shoemaker)\n\nPhase         Dose                 Frequency            Duration\n\nInitial       50 mcg per spray     4 times daily        1 month\n\nMaintenance   100 mcg per spray    4 times daily        Ongoing as needed\n\nSpray into alternating nostrils throughout the day.\n\nALTERNATIVE PROTOCOLS (clinical practice)\n\nSensitive patients:    25 mcg starting dose, titrate up gradually\n\nMaintenance phase:     50 mcg twice daily\n\nCourse length:         1 to 6 months depending on response\n\nPrerequisites (Shoemaker Protocol)\n\nThis sequencing is critically important. VIP should not be started until the patient is in a mold-safe environment (ERMI less than 2 or HERTSMI-2 less than 11), Visual Contrast Sensitivity test is passing, MARCoNS (deep nasal staph) has been treated and confirmed negative on retest, and other protocol steps have been completed. Using VIP while still exposed to biotoxins or harboring nasal colonization can be counterproductive and may explain why some patients report no benefit or worsening of symptoms.\n\nWhy Nasal Spray\n\nThe nasal route offers specific advantages for VIP delivery. It bypasses first-pass liver metabolism so more of the peptide reaches systemic circulation intact. It provides direct delivery to the brain via the olfactory pathway, which is important for the neurological effects. It allows rapid systemic absorption. And it mimics natural VIP distribution patterns more closely than injectable administration.\n\nReconstitution (for research vials)\n\nRECONSTITUTION\n\nVial size:      10 mg\n\nBac water:      2 mL\n\nConcentration:  500 mcg per 10 units on insulin syringe\n\n(or 50 mcg per 1 unit)\n\nNote: Clinical use of VIP for CIRS typically requires a compounded nasal spray obtained through a compounding pharmacy with a prescription. …","frequency":"Twice daily","reconstitution":"Default: follow peptide-specific storage guide. Use bacteriostatic water unless protocol notes require sterile water, saline, or 0.6% acetic acid.","stability":"Use peptide-specific stability notes. Default customer warning: refrigerate after reconstitution, protect from light, do not freeze, and observe the 28-day multi-dose safety clock.","disclaimer":"For research use only. Not medical advice. Not for human or animal consumption.","doses":[{"value":50,"unit":"mcg","mg":0.05}],"strengths":[5,10]}];
